      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Introduction to React\n\n- **React.js** is an open-source JavaScript **library** for building\nuser interfaces, primarily maintained by **Meta** (Facebook) and a\ncommunity of individual developers and companies.\n- **Created By:** Jordan Walke, a software engineer at Facebook.\n- **History:** React Was First deployed on Facebook's News Feed\nin **2011**, and later on Instagram in **2012**. It was open-\nsourced at JSConf US in May **2013**.\n- **Purpose:** Built for creating fast, scalable, and dynamic\n**Single Page Applications (SPAs)**.\n- **Comparison:** Unlike full-fledged frameworks like Angular, React\nfocuses specifically on the **View layer** of the application,\nmaking it lightweight and highly adaptable.\n- It is created by Facebook for there internal application Later\nthey made it open source.\n\n**NOTE :**\n\n**JSX (JavaScript XML)**\n\n- **JSX = JavaScript XML**\n- JSX is a syntax used in React that allows us to write **HTML-\nlike code inside JavaScript**.\n- JSX is a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows you to\nwrite HTML-like markup directly inside JavaScript files.\n- It compiles down to standard `React.createElement()` function\ncalls via Babel.\n- **Single Root Element:**\n    - Every JSX block must return a single top-level element or a\nfragment (`<>...</>`).\n- **JavaScript Expressions:**\n    - Wrap dynamic expressions, variables, or functions inside curly\nbraces (`{expression}`).\n- **CamelCase Attributes:**\n    - Use `className` instead of `class`, `htmlFor` instead of\n`for`, and `onClick` instead of `onclick`.\n- **Self-Closing Tags:**\n    - All standalone tags must be explicitly closed (e.g., `<img />`,\n`<br />`, `<input />`).\n\n### Rule 1: Return only one parent element\n\n❌ Wrong:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <h1>Hello</h1>\n    <p>Welcome</p>\n);\n```\n\n✅ Correct:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <div>\n        <h1>Hello</h1>\n        <p>Welcome</p>\n    </div>\n);\n```\n\nOr use a **Fragment**:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <>\n        <h1>Hello</h1>\n        <p>Welcome</p>\n    </>\n);\n```\n\n# Installation & Tooling or React Folder\n\nTo work with React, you need **Node.js** installed on your\ncomputer.\n\n- **Node.js:**\n    - A JavaScript runtime environment that allows you to execute\nJavaScript outside of a web browser (on the server or local\nsystem).\n\nNOTES :-\n\n- When we install react and any other library we have to use NPM\nor NPX.\n- NPM and NPX it's Comes by default with the node JS\n- **NPM vs. NPX:**\n    - **NPM (Node Package Manager):**\n        - NPM stand for node package manager.\n        - Used to install, manage, and track dependencies/packages\nin your project directory (`node_modules`).\n    - **NPX (Node Package Execute):**\n        - NPX stand for node package execute.\n        - A tool that comes with NPM to execute packages without\nneeding to install them globally or permanently on your disk.\n        - //—\n        - **NPX** is a package runner tool included with Node.js\nthat executes a JavaScript package or CLI tool directly **without\npermanently installing it** on your machine.\n        - A tool bundled with Node.js used to execute npm packages\ndirectly without permanently installing them. It fetches the\nrequired package, executes the command, and leaves\n        your environment clean.\n\n### Modern Project Setup with Vite\n\n- React usages vite as a module bundler.\n- While older setups used `create-react-app`, modern React\ndevelopment uses **Vite** as a build tool and module bundler.\n- **React internally usages Babel.**\n\nBash\n\n```bash\n# Command to create a new React app with Vite using NPX\nnpm create vite@latest my-react-app -- --template react\ncd my-react-app\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n- **Module Bundler (Vite):**\n    - Vite is a modern tools which is used to create, run, and built\napplicaton.\n    - A module Bundler combines all react files (JS, JSX, CSS, HTML,\nImages etc.).so the borwser understand them.\n    - Combines and processes all your modules (JS, JSX, CSS,\nImages) into optimized files that the browser can execute\nefficiently.\n- **Transpiler (Babel):**\n    - It is a **Transpiler** that convert modern js and jsx into\nbrowser understandable javascript\n    - React applications use Babel (or modern alternatives like\nSWC) to convert modern JavaScript (ES6+) and **JSX** into\nbrowser-understandable JavaScript.\n\n## FILES AND FOLDER PRESENT INSIDE THE REACT\nAPPLICATION\n\n- When you create a standard Vite + React project, you get the\nfollowing folder structure:\n\n| **File / Folder** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **`node_modules/`** | Contains all the third-party libraries and\ndependencies installed via NPM. |\n| **`src/`** | The primary source directory where all your React\ncomponents, CSS, and app code live. |\n| **`.gitignore`** | Tells Git which files and folders (e.g.,\n`node_modules`, `.env`) should **not** be tracked or pushed to\nGitHub. |\n| **`package.json`** | Contains metadata about the project,\nscript commands (`dev`, `build`), and the list of installed\ndependencies. |\n| **`package-lock.json`** | Records the exact version of every\ninstalled package to ensure reproducible builds across different\nmachines. |\n| **`README.md`** | Markdown documentation providing details\nabout the project and instructions on how to run it. |\n| **`vite.config.js`** | Configuration file used to customize plugins,\nbuild options, and dev server settings for Vite. |\n| **`eslint.config.js`** | Configuration for ESLint, a linter that\nanalyzes your code to catch syntax errors, missing variables, and\nformatting violations. |\n1. **node_modules/ : -**\n    - It is the folder where all the installed library of a react\nproject is stored.\n    - Contains all the third-party libraries and dependencies\ninstalled via NPM.\n2. **src/ :-**\n    - It is the main folder where all the code are return.\n    - The primary source directory where all your React\ncomponents, CSS, and app code live.\n3. **.gitignore :-**\n    - It is the file that tells the git which files and folder should\nnot to be updated on the GitHub.\n    - Tells Git which files and folders (e.g., `node_modules`,\n`.env`) should **not** be tracked or pushed to GitHub.\n4. **Package.json :-**\n    - It is a files that tell what a project it, which library its needs\nand how to run the project\n    - Contains metadata about the project, script commands\n(`dev`, `build`), and the list of installed dependencies.\n5. **package-lock.json :-** \n    - It is a files that saves exact version of all the installed\nlibraries so, the project works the same on every computer.\n    - Records the exact version of every installed package to\nensure reproducible builds across different machines.\n6. Readme.md :- \n    - this files explain the project details and how to run it.\n    - Markdown documentation providing details about the project\nand instructions on how to run it.\n7. vite.config.js :-\n    - It is a configuration files which is used to customize and\ncontrol the vite behavior for a project.\n    - Configuration file used to customize plugins, build options, and\ndev server settings for Vite.\n8. eslint.config.js :- \n    - Eslint it is the tool that's checks react  told for mistake and\nbad coding practices.\n        - it automatically detect error and enforces coding standard\nin react application\n        - it finds syntax error, missing brackets, wrong variable,\nwon't about unused variable\n    - Configuration for ESLint, a linter that analyzes your code to\ncatch syntax errors, missing variables, and formatting violations.\n\n## Key Features of React\n\n1. Single Page Application (SPA)\n2. Component-Based Architecture\n3. Declarative UI\n4. Virtual DOM\n\n### 1. Single Page Application (SPA)\n\n- Single page Application load a single html file and dynamically\nupdate its content as the user interact with the app this result in\nfaster and smother and transition providing more app like\nexperience\n- This provides a fast, smooth user experience.\n- Instead of requesting entire new HTML pages from the server on\nevery navigation, an SPA loads a single HTML document and\nupdates page content dynamically using JavaScript. This provides a\nfast, smooth user experience.\n\n### 2. Component-Based Architecture\n\n- UIs are broken down into small, independent, and reusable pieces\ncalled **components**.\n- Component based architecture it is a way to build react\narchitecture by braking them into reusable independent peace of\ncode.\n- There are tow type of components we have in react        \n         a.  Class based components\n         b.  Function based components\n\n### 3. Declarative UI\n\n- You describe **what** the UI should look like for a given state,\nand React automatically updates and renders the right components\nwhen your data changes.\n\n### 4. Virtual DOM\n\n- It is  a light weight copy of real DOM.\n- React usages virtual Dom to improves its performance instated\nof updating the actual real dom.\n- React first update the virtual Dom and figure out what changed\nand than only updated is patches to the real dom.\n- //—-\n- The **Virtual DOM (VDOM)** is an in-memory, lightweight\nrepresentation of the real DOM.\n    - Instead of directly manipulating the real DOM (which is slow\nand expensive), React updates the Virtual DOM first.\n    - It calculates the minimal set of changes needed and patches\nonly those specific elements into the real DOM.\n\n# Reconciliation & React Fiber\n\n## Reconciliation :-\n\n- In reconciliation the old virtual Dom  is compare to the new\nvirtual Dom to identify the changes that needs to be updated in\nthe real dom.\n- Reconciliation is the process through which React updates the\nreal DOM based on changes in the Virtual DOM:\n    1. When state or props change, React creates a new Virtual\nDOM tree. \n        1. and than compares it with the previous virtual Dom tree,\nthis compression is called diffing.\n        2. React usages Huffman diffing algorithm to efficiently\ncompare the old virtual Dom tree or newly update Dom tree\n    2. React compares the new Virtual DOM tree with the previous\nVirtual DOM tree using a heuristic **Diffing Algorithm**\n(Operating in $O(n)$ time complexity).\n    3. After calculating the differences, React updates **only**\nthe altered nodes in the real DOM.\n    \n    https://encrypted-\ntbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcQYkfum8kIEn8BXi3VSWNxC\nWXwZZVwgKcFMmK6u8qPnZfYslfHmVzm37Gk&s=10\n    \n\n## React Fiber :-\n\n- It the new  reconciliation engine which is introduce in react 16.\n- It is an improve version of react reconciliation algorithm that\nmakes rendering faster, smother, and uninterruptible.\n- React fibers helps to handle large rendering task into small\nchunks, So, important user enter cation remain fast and smooth.\n- **Key Improvement:**\n    - It introduces **incremental rendering**—the ability to split\nrendering work into small chunks, pause it, and resume it across\nmultiple frames.\n- **Benefit:**\n    - Ensures high-priority user inputs, animations, and transitions\nremain smooth even during large rendering tasks.\n\n## Class-Based vs. Functional Components\n\n> ⚠️ **Note on Modern React:** Class components were the\nstandard in early React versions. Today, **Functional Components\nwith React Hooks** are the universal standard.\n> \n\n| **Class Components** | **Functional Components (Modern)** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Written as ES6 JavaScript Classes | Written as standard\nJavaScript Functions |\n| State Full | StateLess |\n| Life Cycle Methods | No life Cycle Methods |\n|  `this`  keyword | No `this` keyword  |\n| Render() Mehthod | No render Method |\n| Hooks | No Hooks |\n\n### Code Comparison Example\n\n#### Class-Based Component:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { Component } from 'react';\n\nclass Greeting extends Component {\n  render() {\n    return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}!</h1>;\n  }\n}\n\nexport default Greeting;\n```\n\n#### Functional Component (Modern Standard):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction Greeting({ name }) {\n  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;\n}\n\nexport default Greeting;\n```\n\n# JSX Rules & React Fragments\n\n- In React, JSX requires a component to return a **single root\nparent element**.\n- If you try to return adjacent JSX elements without a wrapper,\nReact throws a syntax error.\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// ❌ INVALID: Causes a compilation error\nfunction BrokenComponent(){\n  return (\n    <h1>Title</h1>\n    <p>Paragraph text</p>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## React Fragments (`<Fragment>` or `<>...</>`)\n\n- A **Fragment** in react to use to group multiple elements\nwithout adding an extra node in the Dom tree.  ( HTML `<div>`\nwrapper node to the real DOM tree.)\n- Fragments helps us to avoid extra <div> wrapper.\n- There are two ways for creating Fragments.\n    1.  Explicit `Fragment` Import\n    2. Short Syntax (`<>...</>`)\n\n#### Method 1:  Explicit `Fragment` Import\n\n- *Required if you need to pass a `key` prop while mapping over a\nlist.*\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction Layout(){\n  return (\n    <Fragment>\n      <h1>Header Title</h1>\n      <p>Subtext content without extra DOM node wrappers.</p>\n    <Fragment/>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Layout;\n```\n\n#### Method 2: Short Syntax (`<>...</>`)\n\n- *The standard way when no key prop is required.*\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction Layout(){\n  return (\n    <>\n      <h1>Header Title</h1>\n      <p>Subtext content without extra DOM node wrappers.</p>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Layout;\n```\n\n# 📦   JavaScript Modules & ES6 Import/Export\n\n## Module in JavaScript?\n\n- A **module** in JavaScript is a separate, self-contained file that\ncontains reusable code (variables, functions, classes, or objects)\nthat can be **exported** from one file and **imported** into\nanother.\n    - **Purpose:**\n        - Instead of writing thousands of lines of code in a single\nfile, modules allow you to break your codebase into smaller,\nmanageable, and maintainable files.\n\n## Types of Module Systems in JavaScript\n\n1. **CommonJS(CJS).**\n2. **ES6 Modules(ESM).**\n\n### 1. CommonJS (CJS)\n\n- It is a module system which is used in node JS.\n- **Syntax:**\n    - Uses `module.exports` to export and `require()` to import.\n- **Loading:** Synchronous (loads files at runtime).\n- **Environment:** Historically used in **Node.js** (backend).\n\n#### Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// mathUtils.js (Exporting)\nconst add = (a, b) => a + b;\nmodule.exports = add;\n//module.exports = { add };\n\n// app.js (Importing)\n\n        //const { add } = \n          //or \nconst add = require('./mathUtils');\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // Output: 5\n```\n\n### 2. ES6 Modules (ESM)\n\n- **Environment:** Introduced in **ES6 (ECMAScript 2015)**.\nUsed in modern browsers, React, Vite, and modern Node.js.\n- **Syntax:**\n    - Uses the `import` and `export` keywords.\n- **Types of Exports in ES6 Modules:**\n    1. **Named Export & Import**\n    2. **Default Export & Import**\n\n## ES6 Exports Breakdown\n\n### A. Named Export & Import\n\n- Allows you to export **multiple** variables, functions, or classes\nfrom a single file using their explicit names.\n- It is called named import because you must have to use the\nexact exported name while importing\n- **Rule:** When importing a named export, you **must use curly\nbraces `{ }`** and the **exact name** specified in the export file\n(unless using an alias with `as`).\n\n#### 1. Named Exports Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// utils.js (Exporting multiple named items)\nexport const API_URL = \"https://api.example.com\";\n\nexport function calculateTax(amount){\n  return amount * 0.18;\n}\n\nexport const multiply = (a, b) => a * b;\n```\n\n#### 2. Named Imports Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// main.js (Importing named items using curly braces)\nimport { API_URL, calculateTax, multiply } from './utils.js';\n\nconsole.log(API_URL); // \"https://api.example.com\"\nconsole.log(calculateTax(100)); // 18\n```\n\n#### 3. Named Imports with Aliases (`as`):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { calculateTax as getTax } from './utils.js';\n\nconsole.log(getTax(200)); // 36\n```\n\n### B. Default Export & Import\n\n- Used when a module primary exports **one single main value**\n(e.g., a React component, a main class, or a core function).\n- **Rule 1:** Each file/module can have **only one `default`\nexport**.\n- **Rule 2:** When importing a default export, you **do NOT use\ncurly braces `{ }`**.\n- **Rule 3:** You can rename the imported value to **whatever\nname you choose**.\n\n#### 1. Default Export Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// Button.jsx (Exporting a single React component as default)\nfunction Button(){\n  return <button>Click Me</button>;\n}\n\nexport default Button;\n```\n\n#### 2. Default Import Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// App.jsx (Importing default without curly braces; custom naming\nallowed)\nimport CustomButton from './Button.jsx'; // Renamed from\n'Button' to 'CustomButton'\n\nfunction App(){\n  return <CustomButton />;\n}\n```\n\n//———————————-\n\n## Combining Named and Default Exports in One File\n\nA single file can have **one default export** and **multiple named\nexports** at the same time:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// UserProfile.js\n\n// Named Export 1\nexport const MAX_BIO_LENGTH = 150;\n\n// Named Export 2\nexport function validateUsername(name){\n  return name.length >= 3;\n}\n\n// Default Export\nexport default function UserProfile({ name }){\n  return <h1>User: {name}</h1>;\n}\n```\n\n#### Importing both in another file:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// App.js\nimport UserProfile, { MAX_BIO_LENGTH, validateUsername } from\n'./UserProfile.js';\n```\n\n//———————\n\n## Quick Comparison Summary\n\n| **Feature** | **Named Export** | **Default Export** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Quantity per file** | Multiple allowed | **Only 1** allowed |\n| **Import Syntax** | Uses `{ }` brackets | **No `{ }`\nbrackets** |\n| **Naming on Import** | Must match exact exported name (unless\n`as` is used) | Can be renamed freely on import |\n| **Export Syntax** | `export const name = ...` | `export default\n..` |\n\n# **Hooks**\n\n- **Hooks are special functions provided by React that allow\nfunctional components to use React features such as state,\neffects, context, etc.**\n- Hooks were introduced in **React 16.8**.\n\nSome important Hooks:\n\n| Hook | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `useState()` | Manage state |\n| `useEffect()` | Perform side effects |\n| `useContext()` | Access context |\n| `useRef()` | Store references/values |\n| `useMemo()` | Optimize calculations |\n| `useCallback()` | Optimize functions |\n| `useReducer()` | Manage complex state |\n\n# ⚓ React Hooks: `useState`\n\n## What is `useState`?\n\n- It is a react hook which helps us function based components to\nmake it from stateless to stateful.\n- `useState` is a fundamental **React Hook** that allows\nfunctional components to store, track, and update local\nstate—converting a functional component from **stateless** to\n**stateful**.\n\n## Key Characteristics & Syntax\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nconst [state, setState] = useState(initialValue);\n```\n\n#### NOTE :-\n\n- It accepts one arguments that is initial value.\n- and It returns an array which consists two values.\n    - first value is the variable which holds the initial value\n    - and second value is the upated function which helps to\nupdate the initial value\n1. **Accepts 1 Argument:**\n    - **`initialValue`**:\n        - The starting value for your state (can be a string, number,\nboolean, array, object, or `null`).\n2. **Returns an Array of 2 Elements:**\n    - **`state` (Index 0):**\n        - The state variable holding the current value.\n    - **`setState` (Index 1):**\n        - The updater function used to update the state variable\nand trigger a component re-render.\n\n## How `useState` Works Under the Hood\n\nWhen you call the updater function (`setState`), React:\n\n1. Updates the state variable with the new value.\n2. Triggers a **re-render** of the component.\n3. Updates the Virtual DOM and updates the Real DOM where\nnecessary.\n\n## Code Examples\n\n### 1. Basic Counter Example (Primitives: Numbers)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Counter(){\n  // Array destructuring: count = current value, setCount = updater\nfunction\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Current Count: {count}</p>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count +\n1)}>Increment</button>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count -\n1)}>Decrement</button>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(0)}>Reset</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Counter;\n```\n\n### 2. Form Input Handling (Primitives: Strings)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction InputExample(){\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input\n        type=\"text\"\n        value={text}\n        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}\n        placeholder=\"Type something...\"\n      />\n      <p>You typed: {text}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InputExample;\n```\n\n### 3. Functional State Updates (Best Practice)\n\n- When the new state depends on the **previous state**, pass a\ncallback function to `setState` instead of a direct value to avoid\nstale closure issues during fast re-renders:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// ❌ Not recommended for rapid state changes\nsetCount(count + 1);\n\n// ✅ Recommended: Uses previous state safely\nsetCount((prevCount) => prevCount + 1);\n```\n\n### 4. Updating Objects in State (Immutability Rule)\n\n- In React, **state should never be mutated directly**. Always\npass a new copy of the object using the spread operator (`...`):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction UserProfile(){\n  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alex', age: 25 });\n\n  const updateAge = () => {\n    // Copy existing user properties, then overwrite 'age'\n    setUser((prevUser) => ({\n      ...prevUser,\n      age: prevUser.age + 1\n    }));\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>{user.name} is {user.age} years old.</p>\n      <button onClick={updateAge}>Increase Age</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Important Rules of `useState`\n\n1. **Only Call Hooks at the Top Level:** \n    1. Never call `useState` inside loops, conditions (`if`\nstatements), or nested functions.\n2. **Only Call Hooks from React Functions:**\n    1.  Call them inside React functional components or custom\nhooks, not plain JavaScript functions.\n3. **State Updates are Asynchronous:**\n    1.  React batches state updates for performance optimization.\nYou cannot read the updated state on the immediate line right\nafter calling `setState`.\n\n# **📬 React Props, Props Drilling & Default Props**\n\n## 1. What are Props?\n\n- **Props** stands for **Properties**.\n- it is a mechanisms for passing the data from one components to\nanother components\n- Props is available in both class based components and function\nbased components\n- It is passed just like we passes attributes in the HTML.\n- PROPS are immutable or Uni-directional.\n- They are a read-only mechanism used to pass data from a\n**parent component to a child component**.\n\n### Key Characteristics of Props:\n\n- **Uni-directional Data Flow:**\n    - Props flow in one direction only—top-down from parent to\nchild.\n- **Immutable:**\n    - A child component can **read** props but **cannot modify**\nthem directly.\n- **HTML Attribute Syntax:**\n    - Props are passed to components using a syntax similar to\nstandard HTML attributes.\n- **Availability:**\n    - Available in both functional components (as function\narguments) and class components (via `this.props`).\n\n### Code Example: Passing & Receiving Props\n\n#### Functional Component (Modern Standard using\nDestructuring):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// Child Component\nconst ChildProps = (props) => {\n    let{data1, data2, data3} = props;\n    // console.log(data1);\n    // console.log(data2);\n    // console.log(data3);\n    return(\n        <div>\n            <h1>ChildProps</h1>\n            <h2>String : {data1}</h2>\n            <h2>Array : {data2}</h2>\n            <h3>Object_ID : {data3.id}</h3>\n            <h3>Object_Name : {data3.objName}</h3>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\nexport default ChildProps;\n\n// Parent Component\n\nimport ChildProps from \"./ChildProps\";\n\nconst ParentProp = () =>{\n\n    let str = \"Bhawesh\";\n    let skills = [\"HTML\", \"CSS\", \"JS\"]\n    let obj = {\n        id : 1,\n        objName : \"Kumar\",\n        address : {\n            pin : 813106,\n            street : \"XYZ\"\n        }\n    }\n    return (\n\n        <div>\n            <h1>ParentProp</h1>\n            <ChildProps data = {str} data2 = {skills} data3= {obj}\n/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default ParentProp\n```\n\n## 2. Props Drilling\n\n- Passing the data from one components to nested child\ncomponents is known as PROPS DRILLING.\n- **Props Drilling** occurs when data is passed through multiple\nlayers of nested intermediate components that do **not** actually\nneed the data themselves, just to reach a deeply nested child\ncomponent.\n\n```jsx\n[ App Component (Holds State) ]\n            │\n            ▼  (Passes prop down)\n   [ Navigation Component ]      <-- Doesn't use prop!\n            │\n            ▼  (Passes prop down)\n     [ UserMenu Component ]      <-- Doesn't use prop!\n            │\n            ▼  (Passes prop down)\n    [ UserAvatar Component ]     <-- Needs & Uses prop!\n```\n\n### Disadvantages of Props Drilling:\n\n1. **Unnecessary Maintenance Burden:**\n    1.  If the prop name or structure changes, you have to update\nevery intermediate component in the chain.\n2. **Boilerplate & Clutter:** \n    1. Unused props bloat intermediate components and reduce code\nreadability.\n3. **Re-rendering Issues:**\n    1.  Changes to props force updates down the component tree.\n\n### How to Fix Props Drilling:\n\n- To avoid this unnecessary rerandring components we uses one\nHOOK that is USE CONTAXT (CONTEXT API).\n- To bypass intermediate components, React offers solutions like:\n    - **Context API (`useContext` Hook):**\n        - Provides global state to any deeply nested component\nwithout manually passing props at every level.\n    - **State Management Libraries:**\n        - Redux Toolkit, Zustand, or Jotai for complex global\napplication state.\n    \n    ```jsx\n    //Child\n    \n    const Child = ({data}) => {\n    \n        // console.log(props);\n        // let{data} = props;\n        // console.log(data);\n        return(\n            <>\n                <h1>Child : {data}</h1>\n            </>\n        )\n    }\n    export default Child\n    \n    //----\n    \n    //Parent\n    \n    import Child from \"./Child\"\n    \n    const Parent = (props) =>{\n        // console.log(props);\n    \n        let{str} = props;\n        // console.log(str);\n        return(\n            <>\n                <h1>Parent</h1>\n                <Child data={str}/>\n           </>\n        )\n    }\n    \n    export default Parent\n    \n    //-------\n    \n    //Grand Parent\n    \n    import Parent from \"./Parent\"\n    \n    const GrandParent = ()=>{\n        let str = \"MainUser\"\n        return(\n            <>\n                {/* <h1>GrandParent : {str}</h1> */}\n                <h1>GrandParent</h1>\n                <Parent str={str}/>\n            </>\n        )\n    }\n    \n    export default GrandParent\n    \n    ```\n    \n\n## 3. Default Props\n\n- Default Props are the predefined values that a react\ncomponents usages when a specific props is not provided by the\nparent components.\n- They act like backup values to ensure the components still works\nproperly even if no values are passed.\n- **Default Props** are predefined fallback values used by a\ncomponent when a parent component does not pass a specific prop.\nThey ensure that the UI renders predictably without breaking or\nreturning `undefined`.\n\n### Code expampe 1 :-\n\n```jsx\n//DefaultComponents \n\nimport DefaultProps1 from \"./DefaultProps1\"\n\nconst DefaultComponent = ()=>{\n    let data = \"user1\"\n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>Default Components</h1>\n           <DefaultProps1 abc={data}/>\n            <DefaultProps1/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default DefaultComponent\n\n//-----\n\n//DefaultProps 1 \n\nconst DefaultProps1 = ({abc = \"Guest\"})=>{\n    \n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>DefaultProps1 : {abc} </h1>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\nexport default DefaultProps1\n```\n\n### Code expampe 2:-\n\n```jsx\n//DefaultComponents \n\nimport DefaultProps1 from \"./DefaultProps1\"\nimport DefaultProps2 from \"./DefaultProps2\"\n\nconst DefaultComponent = ()=>{\n    let data = \"user1\"\n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>Default Components</h1>\n            {/* <DefaultProps1 abc={data}/>\n            <DefaultProps1/> */}\n\n            <DefaultProps2 data = {data}/>\n            <DefaultProps2/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default DefaultComponent\n\n//----\n\n//DefaultProps 2 \n\n        // function DefaultProps2(props){\nconst DefaultProps2 = (props)=>{\n    \n    let {data} = props;\n    // let {data = \"Guest\"} = props;\n    \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>DefaultProps2 : {data || \"Guest\"} </h1>\n            {/* <h1>DefaultProps2 : {data} </h1> */}\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default DefaultProps2\n\n```\n\n# 🔄 Component Lifecycle & Phases in React\n\n## What is the React Component Lifecycle?\n\n- React Life Cycle means the different states  a components goes\nthrough.\n- Every React component goes through a series of stages from\nthe moment it is created and inserted into the DOM to the time it\nis destroyed and removed. These stages are known as the\n**Component Lifecycle**.\n\nThe lifecycle is divided into **three primary phases**, plus a\nspecialized **Error Handling** phase.\n\n## The Three Primary Lifecycle Phases\n\n### 1. Mounting Phase (Birth)\n\n- When ever any components render first time in the UI this Phase\nis called mounting Phase.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Occurs when a component is created and inserted into the\nReal DOM for the **first time**.\n- **Common Tasks:**\n    - Setting initial state, making API requests to fetch data,\nsetting up subscriptions/event listeners.\n\n### 2. Updating Phase (Growth)\n\n- When ever in state or Props of a components changes and the\ncomponents gets re-rendering this faces comes under updating\nPhase\n- **Definition:**\n    - Occurs whenever a component’s **`state`** or **`props`**\nchange, causing React to re-render the component and sync\nchanges with the DOM.\n- **Common Tasks:**\n    - Refetching data when props change, updating UI based on\nuser input, running side effects.\n\n### 3. Unmounting Phase (Death)\n\n- When ever the components is removes from the UI this faces is\nknown as unmounting Phase.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Occurs when a component is **removed/deleted** from the\nReal DOM tree.\n- **Common Tasks:**\n    - Cleaning up timers, canceling network requests, removing event\nlisteners to prevent **memory leaks**.\n\n## NOTE :- 4. Special Phase: Error Handling\n\n- Error Handling in life cycle is the process where catches the\nerrors in components and prevents the entire app from crashing\n- React Introduced this feature in REACT 16 with error\nboundaries\n- Introduced in **React 16**, Error Handling uses **Error\nBoundaries** to catch JavaScript errors anywhere in a child\ncomponent tree, log those errors, and display a fallback UI instead\nof crashing the entire application.\n\n> ⚠️ **Note:** Currently, Error Boundaries **must be written as\nClass Components** because there are no functional hook\nequivalents for `componentDidCatch` or\n`getDerivedStateFromError`.\n> \n\n```jsx\n//App.jsx\nimport LifeCycleInCbc from \"./lifeCycle/LifeCycleInCbc\"\nimport { useState } from \"react\"\n\nconst App = () =>{\n\n        let [toggle, setToggle] = useState(false)\n        // // console.log(toggle);\n        \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>App</h1>\n            \n                {/* <LifeCycleInCbc/> */}\n\n            {/* <button onClick={()=>\nsetToggle(!toggle)}>Toggle</button>\n            {toggle ? <LifeCycleInCbc/> : \"No-Component\"} */}\n\n        </div>\n    )\n}\nexport default App\n\n//LifeCycleInCbc.jsx\n\nimport React , {Component} from \"react\";\n\nexport default class LifeCycleInCbc extends Component{\n\n    state = {\n        count : 0\n    }\n\n    componentDidMount(){\n        console.log(\"Mounting Phase\");\n    }\n    componentDidUpdate(){\n        console.log(\"Unmounting Phase\");\n    }\n\n    componentWillUnmount(){\n        console.log(\"Unmounting Phase\");\n    }\n\n    Increment = ()=>{\n        this.setState({count : this.state.count + 1})\n    }\n\n    render(){\n        return(\n            <div>\n                <h1>LifeCycleInCbc : {this.state.count}</h1>\n\n                <button onClick={this.Increment}>Increment</button>\n            </div>\n        )\n    }\n}\n```\n\n# ⚡ React Hooks: `useEffect`\n\n## What is `useEffect`?\n\n- Use Effect is a react hook which helps to handle side effect in\nour react application.\n- This Hook Can also we used for checking the Phases of Function\nBased Components.\n- `useEffect` is a core **React Hook** used to perform **side\neffects** in functional components.\n\n### What is a \"Side Effect\"?\n\n- Side effect live fetching the data from API removing Event-\nListener, Updating DOM etc.\n- A side effect is any operation that affects something outside\nthe scope of the current function execution, such as:\n    - **Fetching data** from an external API (`fetch` / `axios`).\n    - **Setting up or removing** timers (`setInterval`,\n`setTimeout`).\n    - **Directly manipulating** the Real DOM (e.g., updating\n`document.title`).\n    - **Adding or removing** window event listeners (`scroll`,\n`resize`).\n\n#### `useEffect` accepts **two arguments**:\n\n1. **Callback Function:** \n2. **Dependency Array:**\n    1. **Callback Function:** \n        1. Contains the side-effect logic to execute. \n        2. It can optionally return a **cleanup function** to cancel\nsubscriptions, clear timers, or remove event listeners before the\ncomponent re-renders or unmounts.\n    2. **Dependency Array (Optional):**\n        1.  Controls **when** the callback function executes based\non value changes.\n\n## Syntax & Arguments\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  // 1. Effect Code (Side Effect Logic)\n\n  return () => {\n    // 2. Cleanup Function (Optional)\n  };\n}, [dependencies]); // 3. Dependency Array (Optional)\n```\n\nCODE :-\n\n```jsx\n//App.jsx\nimport LifeCycleInFbc from \"./lifeCycle/LifeCycleInFbc\"\nimport { useState } from \"react\"\n\nconst App = () =>{\n\n        // let [toggle, setToggle] = useState(false)\n        // // console.log(toggle);\n        \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>App</h1>\n\n               <LifeCycleInFbc/> \n                \n           <button onClick={()=>\nsetToggle(!toggle)}>Toggle</button>\n            {toggle ? <LifeCycleInFbc/> : \"No-Component\"}\n\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default App\n\n//LifeCycleInFbc.jsx\n\nimport { useEffect, useState } from \"react\";\n\n const LifeCycleInFbc = () =>{\n\n    let [count, setCount] = useState(0)\n\n    useEffect(()=>{\n        console.log(\"Updation Phase\");\n    },[])\n\n    useEffect(()=>{\n        console.log(\"Updation Phase\");\n    },[count])\n\n    useEffect(()=>{\n        return ()=>{\n            console.log(\"Un-Mounting Phase\");\n        }\n    },[])\n\n    return(\n        <div>\n            <h1>LifeCycleInFbc : {count}</h1>\n            <button onClick={()=>setCount(count +\n1)}>Increment</button>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default LifeCycleInFbc\n```\n\n//—————\n**Lifecycle Implementation: Class Components vs. Functional\nComponents (`useEffect`)**\n\n| **Lifecycle Phase** | **Class Component Method** | **Functional\nComponent Equivalent (useEffect)** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Mounting** | `componentDidMount()` | `useEffect(() => { ... },\n[])` *(Empty dependency array)* |\n| **Updating** | `componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState)` |\n`useEffect(() => { ... }, [dependency])` *(With dependencies)* |\n| **Unmounting** | `componentWillUnmount()` | `useEffect(() => {\nreturn () => { ... } }, [])` *(Cleanup function)* |\n| **Error Handling** | `componentDidCatch()` /\n`getDerivedStateFromError()` | Class Component Error Boundary |\n\n# 🎨 React Hooks: `useLayoutEffect`\n\n## What is `useLayoutEffect`?\n\n- Use layout effect is a react hook that runs synchronically after\nthe DOM has been updated, but before the browser paints the\nscreen.\n- `useLayoutEffect` is a version of `useEffect` that fires\n**synchronously** after React has performed all DOM mutations,\nbut **before the browser paints the updated screen to the\nuser**.\n- `useLayoutEffect` accepts the **exact same arguments** as\n`useEffect`:\n- It accepts two arguments :\n    1. CallBack Function :\n    2. Dependency Array :\n        1.  **Callback Function:** \n            1. Contains the DOM manipulation/measurement logic and\noptional cleanup function.\n        2. **Dependency Array:** \n            1. Controls when the effect re-runs.\n\n## Key Difference: `useEffect` vs. `useLayoutEffect`\n\n```\n[ State Changes / Render ]\n           │\n           ▼\n[ React Mutates Virtual DOM ]\n           │\n           ▼\n[ useLayoutEffect Runs ] ───► (Synchronous: Reads/Measures\nDOM before user sees it)\n           │\n           ▼\n[ Browser Paints Screen ]\n           │\n           ▼\n[ useEffect Runs ] ─────────► (Asynchronous: Runs after\nscreen is visible)\n```\n\n| **Feature** | **useEffect** | **useLayoutEffect** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Execution Timing** | **Asynchronous** (Runs *after* browser\npaints screen) | **Synchronous** (Runs *before* browser paints\nscreen) |\n| **User Experience** | Fast, doesn't block painting | Blocks\npainting until execution finishes |\n| **Visual Flickering** | Can cause visible flickering if DOM is\nmodified | **Prevents flickering** by modifying DOM before visual\npaint |\n| **Primary Use Cases** | API fetching, event listeners, state\nupdates | DOM measurements (width/height), tooltip positioning,\nlayout adjustments |\n\n## Syntax & Arguments\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\n  // Synchronous DOM manipulation or measurements\n\n  return () => {\n    // Cleanup logic before unmount/re-render\n  };\n}, [dependencies]);\n```\n\n## When to Use `useLayoutEffect`\n\nUse `useLayoutEffect` **only when you need to read the layout\nfrom the DOM and immediately re-render before the user sees the\nscreen**:\n\n1. **Measuring DOM Elements:** \n    1. Calculating `getBoundingClientRect()`, element width, or\nelement height before rendering tooltips or popups.\n2. **Preventing Visual Flickers:** \n    1. Adjusting scroll positions, element positions, or inline styles\nbefore the browser paints.\n\n> ⚠️ **Performance Warning:** Because `useLayoutEffect` is\nsynchronous, long-running code inside it will **block the browser\nfrom rendering**, causing noticeable page lag. Default to\n`useEffect` whenever possible!\n> \n\n## Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n//App.jsx\n\nimport { useState } from \"react\"\n\nimport UseEffect from \"./UseEffect/UseEffect\"\nimport UseLayout from \"./useLayoutEffect/UseLayout\"\n\nconst App = () =>{\n        \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>App</h1>\n            <UseEffect/>\n            <UseLayout/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default App\n\n// UseLayout\nimport React, { useEffect, useLayoutEffect, useState } from\n'react'\n\nconst UseLayout = () => {\n\n    let[data, setData] = useState(\"JavaScript\")\n\n    // useEffect(()=>{\n    //     setData(\"ReactJS\")\n    // },[]) //runs after the screen paint\n\n    useLayoutEffect(()=>{\n        setData(\"ReactJS\")\n    },[]) //runs before the screen paint\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>UseLayout : {data}</h1>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default UseLayout\n```\n\n# 🎨 Ways of Adding CSS in React\n\nIn React, there are several approaches to style components\ndepending on project size and scoping requirements.\n\n1. Inline CSS\n2. Internal CSS\n3. External CSS\n\n## 1. Inline CSS\n\n- **Definition:**\n    - Writing styles directly on JSX elements using the `style`\nattribute as a **JavaScript object**.\n    - In inline CSS Properties are written in camel case and values\nare written mostly in string.\n- **Syntax Rules:**\n    - Property names are written in **camelCase** (e.g.,\n`backgroundColor` instead of `background-color`).\n    - Property values are typically passed as **strings** (e.g.,\n`'16px'`, `'center'`). Numeric values without units default to pixels\n(`padding: 10` becomes `10px`).\n\n### Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction InlineCard(){\n  // Can be passed directly or as a defined style object\n  const cardStyle = {\n    backgroundColor: '#f4f4f4',\n    padding: '20px',\n    borderRadius: '8px',\n    textAlign: 'center'\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div style={cardStyle}>\n      <h2 style={{ color: '#007bff', fontSize: '24px' }}>Inline\nStyling</h2>\n      <p>Styles are applied directly as JS objects.</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InlineCard;\n```\n\n## 2. Internal CSS (Using `<style>` Tag)\n\n- Internal CSS is define inside the same file or components using a\nstyle tag in JSX.\n- it is used for small components, where CSS is not reused\neverywhere.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Defining CSS rules inside the component file using a `<style>`\ntag within JSX or template strings.\n- **Usage:**\n    - Useful for quick prototyping or isolated small components,\nthough rarely used in large production codebases due to global\nscope leakage unless paired with CSS-in-JS tools.\n\n### Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction InternalCard(){\n  return (\n    <div className=\"internal-container\">\n    \n      <style>{`\n      \n        .internal-container {\n          background-color: #eef2f7;\n          border: 1px solid #d1d5db;\n          padding: 15px;\n          border-radius: 6px;\n        }\n        \n        .internal-container h3 {\n          color: #1f2937;\n        }\n        \n        .example1\n                 {\n                        color : red;\n                        font-size : 100px;\n                        background-color : black;\n                        text-align : center;\n                  }\n                  \n          .example2\n                    {\n                               color : white;\n                        font-size : 100px;\n                        background-color : black;\n                        text-align : center;\n                    }\n        \n      `}\n      </style>\n      \n      <h3 className='example1'>Internal CSS Example</h3>\n      <p className='example2'>Styles defined directly inside a JSX\nstyle block.</p>\n      \n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InternalCard;\n```\n\n## 3. External CSS\n\n- In external CSS we have to create separate CSS file with . CSS\nextension and then we have to import the CSS file in our\ncomponents.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Creating a separate stylesheet with a `.css` extension and\nimporting it directly into the component file using ES6 `import`.\n- **Behavior:**\n    - By default, standard external CSS is **globally scoped**,\nmeaning class names can clash across different components if\nnames overlap.\n\n### Code Example:\n\n**`Card.css`** (External Stylesheet)\n\nCSS\n\n```css\n.external-card {\n  background-color: #ffffff;\n  border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);\n}\n\n.external-card .title {\n  color: #111827;\n  font-size: 1.25rem;\n}\n```\n\n**`Card.jsx`** (Component File)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport './Card.css'; // Import the external stylesheet\n\nfunction Card(){\n  return (\n    <div className=\"external-card\">\n      <h3 className=\"title\">External CSS Example</h3>\n      <p>Imported from an external .css file.</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Card;\n```\n\n# 📦 CSS Modules in React\n\n## What is a CSS Module?\n\n- CSS module allows you to scope CSS locally to a components\navoiding global conflicts.\n- Each class gets a unique generated name.\n- In Big react Project multiple Developer can Style Components\nwithout Overriding Global CSS\n- A **CSS Module** is a CSS file where all class names and\nanimation names are **scoped locally** to the component that\nimports it by default, avoiding **global scope conflicts** and\naccidental style overrides.\n\n## Why Use CSS Modules?\n\n1. **Local Scoping (No Class Name Collisions):**\n    1.  In large React projects with multiple developers, two\ndevelopers can create a class named `.button` or `.container`\nwithout their styles overwriting each other.\n2. **Unique Generated Names:** \n    1. During build time, the bundler (Vite / Webpack) converts your\nclass names into unique hashed strings (e.g., `button` ——>\n`_Button_button_abc123`).\n3. **Seamless Integration:** \n    1. Built into modern build tools like **Vite** and **Create\nReact App** out of the box—no extra configuration needed.\n\n## How It Works (Naming Convention)\n\n- To tell React and the module bundler to treat a stylesheet as a\nmodule, name the file using the format:\n\n$$\n\\text{filename}\\mathbf{.module.css}\n$$\n\n## Step-by-Step Code Example\n\n### 1. Create the Module Stylesheet: `Button.module.css`\n\nCSS\n\n```css\n/* Button.module.css */\n\n.container{\n    background-color: aqua;\n    height: 60vh;\n    width: 100vw;\n    display: flex;\n    justify-content: center;\n    align-items: center;\n}\nform{\n    height: 80%;\n    width: 80%;\n    background-color: white;\n    display: flex;\n    flex-direction: column;\n    justify-content: center;\n    align-items: center;\n    gap: 10px;\n    border-radius: 10px;\n}\n.heading{\n    text-align: center;\n}\n\ninput, #btn{\n    padding: 10px;\n    border-radius: 10px;\n    width: 70%;\n    border: none;\n    border: 1px solid rgb(203,201,201);\n\n}\ninput{\n    border-radius: 5px;\n\n}\n#btn{\n    background-color: aqua;\n    color: #871414;\n    font-weight: bold;\n    font-size: 15px;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Import and Use in Component: `Button.jsx`\n\nImport the CSS module as a JavaScript object (commonly named\n`styles` or `classes`):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// Button.jsx\nimport styles from './Button.module.css';\n\nconst Button = ()=>{\n    return (\n        <div className={style.container}>\n            <form>\n                <h1 className={style.heading}>Login</h1>\n                <input type=\"text\" placeholder='UserName' />\n                <input type=\"password\" placeholder='Password' />\n                <button id={style.btn}> Login </button>\n                \n            </form>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default Button\n```\n\n**Quick Comparison Summary :-**\n\n| **Styling Method** | **Scope** | **Syntax** | **Best Use\nCase** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Inline CSS** | Component / Element | JS Object (`camelCase`) |\nDynamic values calculated at runtime |\n| **Internal CSS** | Global (unless wrapped) | Standard CSS inside\n`<style>` | Quick demos or single-file prototypes |\n| **External CSS** | Global | Standard CSS (`.css` files) | Global\napp styles, resets, typography |\n| **CSS Modules** | **Local / Scoped** | Imported as\n`styles.className` | Component-specific styling in production |\n\n# 🌐 React Context API & `useContext`\n\n## What is Context API?\n\n- CONTEXT API in the react that allows you to share the data\nglobally between different components without passing props\nmanually And each level.\n- Previously before context API we can not share the data\ndirectly to the middle components. To pass the data directly to\nthe middle components we have to used context API.\n- Steps to create CONTEXT API\n    - 1. Create CONTEXT.\n    - 2. CONTEXT Providers && Providing Values.\n    - 3. Use CONTEXT.\n- The **Context API** is a built-in React feature designed to\nshare data (state, functions, theme, user authentication)\n**globally** across an entire component tree without needing to\npass props manually through every intermediate component level\n(**eliminates Props Drilling**).\n\n```jsx\n   Without Context (Props Drilling)           With Context API\n\n        [ App (User State) ]                 [ UserContext.Provider ]\n                 │                                      │\n                 ▼                                      │ (Global Access)\n        [ Dashboard (Prop) ]                            ├──► [ Header\n]\n                 │                                      │\n                 ▼                                      └──► [ UserProfile\n]\n        [ UserProfile (Prop) ]\n```\n\n## —> 3 Core Steps to Implement Context API\n\n1. **`createContext()`**:\n    1.  Creates the context object.\n2. **`Context.Provider`**:\n    1.  Wraps the component tree and supplies the data (`value`\nprop) to all nested children.\n3. **`useContext()`**: \n    1. The hook used by any child component to consume the\nprovided data directly.\n\n**CODE EXAMPLE 1 :** \n\n```jsx\n//MyContext.jsx\n\nimport React, { createContext } from 'react'\nconst MyContext = createContext();\nexport default MyContext\n\n// ContextParnet.jsx\n\nimport ContextChild1 from \"./ContextChild1\"\nimport MyContext from \"./MyContext\";\n\nconst ContextParent = () => {\n\n    let data1 = \"Global Data\";\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextParent</h1>\n        <MyContext.Provider value = {data1}>\n\n            <ContextChild1/>\n\n           </MyContext.Provider>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\nexport default ContextParent\n\n//ContextChild1.jsx\n\nimport ContextChild2 from \"./ContextChild2\"\n\nconst ContextChild1 = () => {\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextChild1</h1>\n        <ContextChild2/>\n    </div>\n\n  )\n}\nexport default ContextChild1\n\n// ContextChild2.jsx\n\nimport { useContext } from \"react\"\nimport MyContext from \"./MyContext\"\n\nconst ContextChild2 = () => {\n\n    let data = useContext(MyContext);\n    console.log(data);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextChild2 : {data}</h1>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextChild2\n\n```\n\n**CODE EXAMPLE 2 :** \n\n```jsx\n//ContextGrandParnet.jsx\n\nimport { createContext } from \"react\"\nimport ContextParent2 from \"./ContextParent2\"\n\n  //! step 1:\n\n   export let MyContext2 = createContext();\n\nconst ContextGrandParent = () => {\n\n    let data2 = \"Global Data 2\"\n    let data3 = \"Global Data 3\"\n    let data4 = \"Global Data 4\"\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextGrandParent</h1>\n\n        {/* //! step 2: */}\n\n        <MyContext2.Provider value = {{data2, data3, data4}}>\n\n            <ContextParent2/>\n\n        </MyContext2.Provider>\n\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextGrandParent\n\n//ContextParnet2.jsx\n\nimport ContextChild from \"./ContextChild\"\n\nconst ContextParent2 = () => {\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextParent2</h1>\n        <ContextChild/>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextParent2\n\n// ContextChild.jsx\n\nimport React, { useContext } from 'react'\nimport {MyContext2} from './ContextGrandParent'\n\nconst ContextChild = () => {\n\n    let ContextData = useContext(MyContext2)\n    let {data2,data3,data4} = ContextData\n    // console.log(ContextData);\n    \n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextChild : {data2}  </h1>\n        <h1>ContextChild : {data3}  </h1>\n        <h1>ContextChild : {data4}  </h1>\n\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextChild\n```\n\n# **⚙️ React Hooks: `useReducer`**\n\n## What is `useReducer`?\n\n- Use Reducer is a react Hooks which is used to handle complex\nstate logic\n- It accepts two arguments :\n    - **Reducer Function**\n    - **Initial State**\n- It is an alternative to `useState` that follows the Redux-style\npattern: actions trigger state transitions via a centralized reducer\nfunction.\n\n## Hook Syntax & Arguments\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nconst [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);\n```\n\n## 1.  `Reducer Function`:\n\n- It is the function that decided how state changes\n- **`reducer` (Reducer Function):** A custom function that\ndetermines how the state transitions.\n- It accepts two arguments:\n    \n    #### `a. state`:\n    \n    - The current/previous state.\n    \n    #### `b. action`:  ****\n    \n    - **An object dispatched to request a state change (typically\nhas a `type` and optional `payload`).**\n- *Returns:*  It returns a new Updated State.\n\n## 2.  `Initial State`:\n\n- It is also known as initial value that we wants our state should\nHold.\n- The starting value/object that the state holds.\n- It returns an array which consists two values.\n    1.  Variable which hold the current state.\n    2.  Function which is known as this Dispatcher function\n    - **`current state` (Index 0):**\n        - The current state variable.\n    - **`dispatch fn` (Index 1):**\n        - The dispatch function used to trigger actions and send\nthem to the reducer function.\n\n## How `useReducer` Works Under the Hood\n\n```jsx\n[ User Interaction ] ──► dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n[ Reducer Function ] ──► reducer(currentState, action)\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n[ New State Returned ] ─► Component Re-renders with Updated\nState\n```\n\n## Code Examples\n\n### 1. Simple Counter Example\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useReducer } from 'react';\n\n// 1. Define Initial State\nconst initialState = { count: 0 };\n\n// 2. Define Reducer Function\nfunction counterReducer(state, action){\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case 'INCREMENT':\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\n    case 'DECREMENT':\n      return { count: state.count - 1 };\n    case 'RESET':\n      return { count: 0 };\n    default:\n      throw new Error(`Unhandled action type: ${action.type}`);\n  }\n}\n\n// 3. Component\nexport default function Counter(){\n  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(counterReducer,\ninitialState);\n\n  return (\n    <div style={{ padding: '20px', fontFamily: 'sans-serif' }}>\n      <h2>Count: {state.count}</h2>\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT'\n})}>Increment</button>\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'DECREMENT'\n})}>Decrement</button>\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'RESET'\n})}>Reset</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## When to Use `useState` vs. `useReducer`\n\n| **Criteria** | **useState** | **useReducer** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Data Type** | Primitives (`number`, `string`, `boolean`) |\nComplex objects, arrays, nested state |\n| **State Transitions** | Simple, independent updates (1–2 values)\n| Multiple sub-values or interdependent state logic |\n| **Business Logic** | Kept directly inside event handlers |\nCentralized inside a single, pure reducer function |\n| **Testing** | Harder to test state logic in isolation | Pure\nreducer functions are easy to unit-test independently |\n\n# 🎛️ Controlled vs. Uncontrolled Components & `useRef`\n\n- Forms are essential in web applications. In React, there are two\ndistinct paradigms for handling form input elements (`<input>`,\n`<textarea>`, `<select>`): **Controlled Components** and\n**Uncontrolled Components**.\n\n## 1. Controlled Components\n\n- A control components is a components in which the input elements\nget their values from react State and any changes in the input is\nhandle through event handler like onChange.\n- **Definition:**\n    - A component where the form input element's value is **driven\nand controlled by React state** (`useState`).\n- **Mechanism:**\n    1. The input's `value` attribute is bound to a state variable.\n    2. Any keystroke or user input triggers an `onChange` event\nhandler to update that state.\n- **Source of Truth:**\n    - **React State** is the single source of truth.\n- **Best Used For:**\n    - Real-time form validation, disabling submit buttons\nconditionally, dynamic inputs, or instant UI updates.\n\n### Controlled Component Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n//---------------------- EX 1\n\nimport React, { useState } from 'react'\n\nconst Controlled = () => {\n\n        let [formData, setFormData] = useState({\n\n            userName : \"\",\n            password : \"\",\n            email : \"\"\n        })\n\n        function handleSubmit(e){\n            e.preventDefault()\n\n            console.log(\"Form Submitted\");\n        }\n\n        function handleChange(e){\n            // console.log(e);\n\n            let{name,value} = e.target\n\n            setFormData ({...formData, [name]:value})\n            console.log(formData);\n            \n            console.log(\"Change\");\n        }\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>Controlled Components</h1>\n\n        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n\n            <input type=\"text\" value = {FormData.userName}\nonChange = {handleChange} name = \"userName\" />\n            \n            <input type=\"password\" value = {FormData.password}\nonChange={handleChange} name = \"password\"/>\n            \n            <input type=\"email\" value ={FormData.email}\nonChange={handleChange} name = \"email\"/>\n\n            <button>Submit</button>\n        </form>\n\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default Controlled\n\n//---------------------- Ex 2 ONLINE\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nexport default function ControlledForm(){\n  const [name, setName] = useState('');\n\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    alert(`Submitted name: ${name}`);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n      <label>Controlled Input: </label>\n      <input\n       type=\"text\" value={name} onChange={(e) =>\nsetName(e.target.value)} placeholder=\"Type here...\" />\n      <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>\n      <p>Live Preview: {name}</p>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Uncontrolled Components\n\n- An Uncontrol components in which input elements manages their\nown data using the Browsers DOM and react access the values\nonly when required using ref.\n- \n- **Definition:**\n    - A component where the form input element manages its own\ninternal state using the **browser's DOM**.\n- **Mechanism:**\n    - React does not track every keystroke. Instead, React pulls\nthe current value directly from the DOM on demand (e.g., when the\nuser clicks \"Submit\") using a **`useRef`** reference.\n- **Source of Truth:**\n    - The **Browser's Real DOM**.\n- **Best Used For:**\n    - Simple forms that only need values on submit, file input fields\n(`<input type=\"file\" />`), or integrating with third-party non-React\nDOM libraries.\n\n### Uncontrolled Component Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```\nimport { useRef } from 'react';\n\nexport default function UncontrolledForm(){\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\n\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    // Access value directly from the DOM element reference\n    alert(`Submitted name: ${inputRef.current.value}`);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n      <label>Uncontrolled Input: </label>\n      <input\n        type=\"text\"\n        ref={inputRef}\n        defaultValue=\"\" // Sets initial value without controlling\nupdates\n        placeholder=\"Type here...\"\n      />\n      <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Summary Comparison: Controlled vs. Uncontrolled\n\n| **Feature** | **Controlled Component** | **Uncontrolled\nComponent** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Data Handler** | React State (`useState`) | Real DOM\n(`useRef`) |\n| **Updates On** | Every keystroke (`onChange`) | On demand /\nform submit |\n| **Instant Validation** | Easy (available in state on every change)\n| Harder (requires manual DOM queries) |\n| **Re-renders** | Re-renders on every keystroke | Does **not**\nre-render on typing |\n| **Standard Recommendation** | **React Recommended** | Useful\nfor edge cases & file uploads |\n\n## 3. The `useRef` Hook\n\n### What is `useRef`?\n\n`useRef` is a built-in React Hook that returns a mutable reference\nobject whose **`.current`** property holds a persisted value\nacross renders.\n\n### Two Core Use Cases of `useRef`:\n\n1. **Accessing and Manipulating DOM Elements Directly:** Focusing\nan input, scrolling to a section, measuring element dimensions, or\nplaying/pausing media.\n2. **Storing Mutable Values Without Causing Re-renders:** Unlike\n`useState`, updating `ref.current` **does not trigger a\ncomponent re-render**.\n\n### Code Examples for `useRef`\n\n#### Use Case 1: Focusing an Input on Button Click (DOM\nAccess)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```\nimport { useRef } from 'react';\n\nexport default function AutoFocusInput(){\n  const inputElement = useRef(null);\n\n  const handleFocus = () => {\n    // Directly focus the input element via the DOM node\n    inputElement.current.focus();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input ref={inputElement} type=\"text\" placeholder=\"Click\nbutton to focus me\" />\n      <button onClick={handleFocus}>Focus Input</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n#### Use Case 2: Persisting Values Without Re-rendering (Timer\n/ Counter)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```\nimport { useState, useRef } from 'react';\n\nexport default function StopWatch(){\n  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);\n  const timerId = useRef(null); // Holds timer ID without triggering\nre-renders\n\n  const startTimer = () => {\n    if (timerId.current !== null) return;\n    timerId.current = setInterval(() => {\n      setSeconds((prev) => prev + 1);\n    }, 1000);\n  };\n\n  const stopTimer = () => {\n    clearInterval(timerId.current);\n    timerId.current = null;\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Seconds: {seconds}</h2>\n      <button onClick={startTimer}>Start</button>\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## `useState` vs. `useRef`\n\n| **Feature** | **useState** | **useRef** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Triggers Re-render?** | **Yes** (when state updater is called)\n| **No** (changing `.current` never re-renders) |\n| **Access Syntax** | `stateVariable` | `refVariable.current` |\n| **Primary Purpose** | Visual UI data that dictates component\nrendering | DOM elements & behind-the-scenes mutable values\n(timers, previous values) |",
      "fontSize": 19.83116426467107,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Introduction to React\n\n- **React.js** is an open-source JavaScript **library** for building user interfaces, primarily maintained by **Meta** (Facebook) and a community of individual developers and companies.\n- **Created By:** Jordan Walke, a software engineer at Facebook.\n- **History:** React Was First deployed on Facebook's News Feed in **2011**, and later on Instagram in **2012**. It was open-sourced at JSConf US in May **2013**.\n- **Purpose:** Built for creating fast, scalable, and dynamic **Single Page Applications (SPAs)**.\n- **Comparison:** Unlike full-fledged frameworks like Angular, React focuses specifically on the **View layer** of the application, making it lightweight and highly adaptable.\n- It is created by Facebook for there internal application Later they made it open source.\n\n**NOTE :**\n\n**JSX (JavaScript XML)**\n\n- **JSX = JavaScript XML**\n- JSX is a syntax used in React that allows us to write **HTML-like code inside JavaScript**.\n- JSX is a syntax extension for JavaScript that allows you to write HTML-like markup directly inside JavaScript files.\n- It compiles down to standard `React.createElement()` function calls via Babel.\n- **Single Root Element:**\n    - Every JSX block must return a single top-level element or a fragment (`<>...</>`).\n- **JavaScript Expressions:**\n    - Wrap dynamic expressions, variables, or functions inside curly braces (`{expression}`).\n- **CamelCase Attributes:**\n    - Use `className` instead of `class`, `htmlFor` instead of `for`, and `onClick` instead of `onclick`.\n- **Self-Closing Tags:**\n    - All standalone tags must be explicitly closed (e.g., `<img />`, `<br />`, `<input />`).\n\n### Rule 1: Return only one parent element\n\n❌ Wrong:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <h1>Hello</h1>\n    <p>Welcome</p>\n);\n```\n\n✅ Correct:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <div>\n        <h1>Hello</h1>\n        <p>Welcome</p>\n    </div>\n);\n```\n\nOr use a **Fragment**:\n\n```jsx\nreturn (\n    <>\n        <h1>Hello</h1>\n        <p>Welcome</p>\n    </>\n);\n```\n\n# Installation & Tooling or React Folder\n\nTo work with React, you need **Node.js** installed on your computer.\n\n- **Node.js:**\n    - A JavaScript runtime environment that allows you to execute JavaScript outside of a web browser (on the server or local system).\n\nNOTES :-\n\n- When we install react and any other library we have to use NPM or NPX.\n- NPM and NPX it's Comes by default with the node JS\n- **NPM vs. NPX:**\n    - **NPM (Node Package Manager):**\n        - NPM stand for node package manager.\n        - Used to install, manage, and track dependencies/packages in your project directory (`node_modules`).\n    - **NPX (Node Package Execute):**\n        - NPX stand for node package execute.\n        - A tool that comes with NPM to execute packages without needing to install them globally or permanently on your disk.\n        - //—\n        - **NPX** is a package runner tool included with Node.js that executes a JavaScript package or CLI tool directly **without permanently installing it** on your machine.\n        - A tool bundled with Node.js used to execute npm packages directly without permanently installing them. It fetches the required package, executes the command, and leaves\n        your environment clean.\n\n### Modern Project Setup with Vite\n\n- React usages vite as a module bundler.\n- While older setups used `create-react-app`, modern React development uses **Vite** as a build tool and module bundler.\n- **React internally usages Babel.**\n\nBash\n\n```bash\n# Command to create a new React app with Vite using NPX\nnpm create vite@latest my-react-app -- --template react\ncd my-react-app\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n```\n\n- **Module Bundler (Vite):**\n    - Vite is a modern tools which is used to create, run, and built applicaton.\n    - A module Bundler combines all react files (JS, JSX, CSS, HTML, Images etc.).so the borwser understand them.\n    - Combines and processes all your modules (JS, JSX, CSS, Images) into optimized files that the browser can execute efficiently.\n- **Transpiler (Babel):**\n    - It is a **Transpiler** that convert modern js and jsx into browser understandable javascript\n    - React applications use Babel (or modern alternatives like SWC) to convert modern JavaScript (ES6+) and **JSX** into browser-understandable JavaScript.\n\n## FILES AND FOLDER PRESENT INSIDE THE REACT APPLICATION\n\n- When you create a standard Vite + React project, you get the following folder structure:\n\n| **File / Folder** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **`node_modules/`** | Contains all the third-party libraries and dependencies installed via NPM. |\n| **`src/`** | The primary source directory where all your React components, CSS, and app code live. |\n| **`.gitignore`** | Tells Git which files and folders (e.g., `node_modules`, `.env`) should **not** be tracked or pushed to GitHub. |\n| **`package.json`** | Contains metadata about the project, script commands (`dev`, `build`), and the list of installed dependencies. |\n| **`package-lock.json`** | Records the exact version of every installed package to ensure reproducible builds across different machines. |\n| **`README.md`** | Markdown documentation providing details about the project and instructions on how to run it. |\n| **`vite.config.js`** | Configuration file used to customize plugins, build options, and dev server settings for Vite. |\n| **`eslint.config.js`** | Configuration for ESLint, a linter that analyzes your code to catch syntax errors, missing variables, and formatting violations. |\n1. **node_modules/ : -**\n    - It is the folder where all the installed library of a react project is stored.\n    - Contains all the third-party libraries and dependencies installed via NPM.\n2. **src/ :-**\n    - It is the main folder where all the code are return.\n    - The primary source directory where all your React components, CSS, and app code live.\n3. **.gitignore :-**\n    - It is the file that tells the git which files and folder should not to be updated on the GitHub.\n    - Tells Git which files and folders (e.g., `node_modules`, `.env`) should **not** be tracked or pushed to GitHub.\n4. **Package.json :-**\n    - It is a files that tell what a project it, which library its needs and how to run the project\n    - Contains metadata about the project, script commands (`dev`, `build`), and the list of installed dependencies.\n5. **package-lock.json :-** \n    - It is a files that saves exact version of all the installed libraries so, the project works the same on every computer.\n    - Records the exact version of every installed package to ensure reproducible builds across different machines.\n6. Readme.md :- \n    - this files explain the project details and how to run it.\n    - Markdown documentation providing details about the project and instructions on how to run it.\n7. vite.config.js :-\n    - It is a configuration files which is used to customize and control the vite behavior for a project.\n    - Configuration file used to customize plugins, build options, and dev server settings for Vite.\n8. eslint.config.js :- \n    - Eslint it is the tool that's checks react  told for mistake and bad coding practices.\n        - it automatically detect error and enforces coding standard in react application\n        - it finds syntax error, missing brackets, wrong variable, won't about unused variable\n    - Configuration for ESLint, a linter that analyzes your code to catch syntax errors, missing variables, and formatting violations.\n\n## Key Features of React\n\n1. Single Page Application (SPA)\n2. Component-Based Architecture\n3. Declarative UI\n4. Virtual DOM\n\n### 1. Single Page Application (SPA)\n\n- Single page Application load a single html file and dynamically update its content as the user interact with the app this result in faster and smother and transition providing more app like experience\n- This provides a fast, smooth user experience.\n- Instead of requesting entire new HTML pages from the server on every navigation, an SPA loads a single HTML document and updates page content dynamically using JavaScript. This provides a fast, smooth user experience.\n\n### 2. Component-Based Architecture\n\n- UIs are broken down into small, independent, and reusable pieces called **components**.\n- Component based architecture it is a way to build react architecture by braking them into reusable independent peace of code.\n- There are tow type of components we have in react        \n         a.  Class based components\n         b.  Function based components\n\n### 3. Declarative UI\n\n- You describe **what** the UI should look like for a given state, and React automatically updates and renders the right components when your data changes.\n\n### 4. Virtual DOM\n\n- It is  a light weight copy of real DOM.\n- React usages virtual Dom to improves its performance instated of updating the actual real dom.\n- React first update the virtual Dom and figure out what changed and than only updated is patches to the real dom.\n- //—-\n- The **Virtual DOM (VDOM)** is an in-memory, lightweight representation of the real DOM.\n    - Instead of directly manipulating the real DOM (which is slow and expensive), React updates the Virtual DOM first.\n    - It calculates the minimal set of changes needed and patches only those specific elements into the real DOM.\n\n# Reconciliation & React Fiber\n\n## Reconciliation :-\n\n- In reconciliation the old virtual Dom  is compare to the new virtual Dom to identify the changes that needs to be updated in the real dom.\n- Reconciliation is the process through which React updates the real DOM based on changes in the Virtual DOM:\n    1. When state or props change, React creates a new Virtual DOM tree. \n        1. and than compares it with the previous virtual Dom tree, this compression is called diffing.\n        2. React usages Huffman diffing algorithm to efficiently compare the old virtual Dom tree or newly update Dom tree\n    2. React compares the new Virtual DOM tree with the previous Virtual DOM tree using a heuristic **Diffing Algorithm**  (Operating in $O(n)$ time complexity).\n    3. After calculating the differences, React updates **only** the altered nodes in the real DOM.\n    \n    https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcQYkfum8kIEn8BXi3VSWNxCWXwZZVwgKcFMmK6u8qPnZfYslfHmVzm37Gk&s=10\n    \n\n## React Fiber :-\n\n- It the new  reconciliation engine which is introduce in react 16.\n- It is an improve version of react reconciliation algorithm that makes rendering faster, smother, and uninterruptible.\n- React fibers helps to handle large rendering task into small chunks, So, important user enter cation remain fast and smooth.\n- **Key Improvement:**\n    - It introduces **incremental rendering**—the ability to split rendering work into small chunks, pause it, and resume it across multiple frames.\n- **Benefit:**\n    - Ensures high-priority user inputs, animations, and transitions remain smooth even during large rendering tasks.\n\n## Class-Based vs. Functional Components\n\n> ⚠️ **Note on Modern React:** Class components were the standard in early React versions. Today, **Functional Components with React Hooks** are the universal standard.\n> \n\n| **Class Components** | **Functional Components (Modern)** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Written as ES6 JavaScript Classes | Written as standard JavaScript Functions |\n| State Full | StateLess |\n| Life Cycle Methods | No life Cycle Methods |\n|  `this`  keyword | No `this` keyword  |\n| Render() Mehthod | No render Method |\n| Hooks | No Hooks |\n\n### Code Comparison Example\n\n#### Class-Based Component:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { Component } from 'react';\n\nclass Greeting extends Component {\n  render() {\n    return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}!</h1>;\n  }\n}\n\nexport default Greeting;\n```\n\n#### Functional Component (Modern Standard):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction Greeting({ name }) {\n  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;\n}\n\nexport default Greeting;\n```\n\n# JSX Rules & React Fragments\n\n- In React, JSX requires a component to return a **single root parent element**.\n- If you try to return adjacent JSX elements without a wrapper, React throws a syntax error.\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// ❌ INVALID: Causes a compilation error\nfunction BrokenComponent(){\n  return (\n    <h1>Title</h1>\n    <p>Paragraph text</p>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## React Fragments (`<Fragment>` or `<>...</>`)\n\n- A **Fragment** in react to use to group multiple elements without adding an extra node in the Dom tree.  ( HTML `<div>` wrapper node to the real DOM tree.)\n- Fragments helps us to avoid extra <div> wrapper.\n- There are two ways for creating Fragments.\n    1.  Explicit `Fragment` Import\n    2. Short Syntax (`<>...</>`)\n\n#### Method 1:  Explicit `Fragment` Import\n\n- *Required if you need to pass a `key` prop while mapping over a list.*\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction Layout(){\n  return (\n    <Fragment>\n      <h1>Header Title</h1>\n      <p>Subtext content without extra DOM node wrappers.</p>\n    <Fragment/>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Layout;\n```\n\n#### Method 2: Short Syntax (`<>...</>`)\n\n- *The standard way when no key prop is required.*\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction Layout(){\n  return (\n    <>\n      <h1>Header Title</h1>\n      <p>Subtext content without extra DOM node wrappers.</p>\n    </>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Layout;\n```\n\n# 📦   JavaScript Modules & ES6 Import/Export\n\n## Module in JavaScript?\n\n- A **module** in JavaScript is a separate, self-contained file that contains reusable code (variables, functions, classes, or objects) that can be **exported** from one file and **imported** into another.\n    - **Purpose:**\n        - Instead of writing thousands of lines of code in a single file, modules allow you to break your codebase into smaller, manageable, and maintainable files.\n\n## Types of Module Systems in JavaScript\n\n1. **CommonJS(CJS).**\n2. **ES6 Modules(ESM).**\n\n### 1. CommonJS (CJS)\n\n- It is a module system which is used in node JS.\n- **Syntax:**\n    - Uses `module.exports` to export and `require()` to import.\n- **Loading:** Synchronous (loads files at runtime).\n- **Environment:** Historically used in **Node.js** (backend).\n\n#### Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// mathUtils.js (Exporting)\nconst add = (a, b) => a + b;\nmodule.exports = add;\n//module.exports = { add };\n\n// app.js (Importing)\n\n        //const { add } = \n          //or \nconst add = require('./mathUtils');\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // Output: 5\n```\n\n### 2. ES6 Modules (ESM)\n\n- **Environment:** Introduced in **ES6 (ECMAScript 2015)**. Used in modern browsers, React, Vite, and modern Node.js.\n- **Syntax:**\n    - Uses the `import` and `export` keywords.\n- **Types of Exports in ES6 Modules:**\n    1. **Named Export & Import**\n    2. **Default Export & Import**\n\n## ES6 Exports Breakdown\n\n### A. Named Export & Import\n\n- Allows you to export **multiple** variables, functions, or classes from a single file using their explicit names.\n- It is called named import because you must have to use the exact exported name while importing\n- **Rule:** When importing a named export, you **must use curly braces `{ }`** and the **exact name** specified in the export file (unless using an alias with `as`).\n\n#### 1. Named Exports Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// utils.js (Exporting multiple named items)\nexport const API_URL = \"https://api.example.com\";\n\nexport function calculateTax(amount){\n  return amount * 0.18;\n}\n\nexport const multiply = (a, b) => a * b;\n```\n\n#### 2. Named Imports Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// main.js (Importing named items using curly braces)\nimport { API_URL, calculateTax, multiply } from './utils.js';\n\nconsole.log(API_URL); // \"https://api.example.com\"\nconsole.log(calculateTax(100)); // 18\n```\n\n#### 3. Named Imports with Aliases (`as`):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { calculateTax as getTax } from './utils.js';\n\nconsole.log(getTax(200)); // 36\n```\n\n### B. Default Export & Import\n\n- Used when a module primary exports **one single main value** (e.g., a React component, a main class, or a core function).\n- **Rule 1:** Each file/module can have **only one `default` export**.\n- **Rule 2:** When importing a default export, you **do NOT use curly braces `{ }`**.\n- **Rule 3:** You can rename the imported value to **whatever name you choose**.\n\n#### 1. Default Export Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// Button.jsx (Exporting a single React component as default)\nfunction Button(){\n  return <button>Click Me</button>;\n}\n\nexport default Button;\n```\n\n#### 2. Default Import Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// App.jsx (Importing default without curly braces; custom naming allowed)\nimport CustomButton from './Button.jsx'; // Renamed from 'Button' to 'CustomButton'\n\nfunction App(){\n  return <CustomButton />;\n}\n```\n\n//———————————-\n\n## Combining Named and Default Exports in One File\n\nA single file can have **one default export** and **multiple named exports** at the same time:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// UserProfile.js\n\n// Named Export 1\nexport const MAX_BIO_LENGTH = 150;\n\n// Named Export 2\nexport function validateUsername(name){\n  return name.length >= 3;\n}\n\n// Default Export\nexport default function UserProfile({ name }){\n  return <h1>User: {name}</h1>;\n}\n```\n\n#### Importing both in another file:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// App.js\nimport UserProfile, { MAX_BIO_LENGTH, validateUsername } from './UserProfile.js';\n```\n\n//———————\n\n## Quick Comparison Summary\n\n| **Feature** | **Named Export** | **Default Export** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Quantity per file** | Multiple allowed | **Only 1** allowed |\n| **Import Syntax** | Uses `{ }` brackets | **No `{ }` brackets** |\n| **Naming on Import** | Must match exact exported name (unless `as` is used) | Can be renamed freely on import |\n| **Export Syntax** | `export const name = ...` | `export default ..` |\n\n# **Hooks**\n\n- **Hooks are special functions provided by React that allow functional components to use React features such as state, effects, context, etc.**\n- Hooks were introduced in **React 16.8**.\n\nSome important Hooks:\n\n| Hook | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `useState()` | Manage state |\n| `useEffect()` | Perform side effects |\n| `useContext()` | Access context |\n| `useRef()` | Store references/values |\n| `useMemo()` | Optimize calculations |\n| `useCallback()` | Optimize functions |\n| `useReducer()` | Manage complex state |\n\n# ⚓ React Hooks: `useState`\n\n## What is `useState`?\n\n- It is a react hook which helps us function based components to make it from stateless to stateful.\n- `useState` is a fundamental **React Hook** that allows functional components to store, track, and update local state—converting a functional component from **stateless** to **stateful**.\n\n## Key Characteristics & Syntax\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nconst [state, setState] = useState(initialValue);\n```\n\n#### NOTE :-\n\n- It accepts one arguments that is initial value.\n- and It returns an array which consists two values.\n    - first value is the variable which holds the initial value\n    - and second value is the upated function which helps to update the initial value\n1. **Accepts 1 Argument:**\n    - **`initialValue`**:\n        - The starting value for your state (can be a string, number, boolean, array, object, or `null`).\n2. **Returns an Array of 2 Elements:**\n    - **`state` (Index 0):**\n        - The state variable holding the current value.\n    - **`setState` (Index 1):**\n        - The updater function used to update the state variable and trigger a component re-render.\n\n## How `useState` Works Under the Hood\n\nWhen you call the updater function (`setState`), React:\n\n1. Updates the state variable with the new value.\n2. Triggers a **re-render** of the component.\n3. Updates the Virtual DOM and updates the Real DOM where necessary.\n\n## Code Examples\n\n### 1. Basic Counter Example (Primitives: Numbers)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Counter(){\n  // Array destructuring: count = current value, setCount = updater function\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>Current Count: {count}</p>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count - 1)}>Decrement</button>\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(0)}>Reset</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Counter;\n```\n\n### 2. Form Input Handling (Primitives: Strings)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction InputExample(){\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input\n        type=\"text\"\n        value={text}\n        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}\n        placeholder=\"Type something...\"\n      />\n      <p>You typed: {text}</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InputExample;\n```\n\n### 3. Functional State Updates (Best Practice)\n\n- When the new state depends on the **previous state**, pass a callback function to `setState` instead of a direct value to avoid stale closure issues during fast re-renders:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// ❌ Not recommended for rapid state changes\nsetCount(count + 1);\n\n// ✅ Recommended: Uses previous state safely\nsetCount((prevCount) => prevCount + 1);\n```\n\n### 4. Updating Objects in State (Immutability Rule)\n\n- In React, **state should never be mutated directly**. Always pass a new copy of the object using the spread operator (`...`):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction UserProfile(){\n  const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alex', age: 25 });\n\n  const updateAge = () => {\n    // Copy existing user properties, then overwrite 'age'\n    setUser((prevUser) => ({\n      ...prevUser,\n      age: prevUser.age + 1\n    }));\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <p>{user.name} is {user.age} years old.</p>\n      <button onClick={updateAge}>Increase Age</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Important Rules of `useState`\n\n1. **Only Call Hooks at the Top Level:** \n    1. Never call `useState` inside loops, conditions (`if` statements), or nested functions.\n2. **Only Call Hooks from React Functions:**\n    1.  Call them inside React functional components or custom hooks, not plain JavaScript functions.\n3. **State Updates are Asynchronous:**\n    1.  React batches state updates for performance optimization. You cannot read the updated state on the immediate line right after calling `setState`.\n\n# **📬 React Props, Props Drilling & Default Props**\n\n## 1. What are Props?\n\n- **Props** stands for **Properties**.\n- it is a mechanisms for passing the data from one components to another components\n- Props is available in both class based components and function based components\n- It is passed just like we passes attributes in the HTML.\n- PROPS are immutable or Uni-directional.\n- They are a read-only mechanism used to pass data from a **parent component to a child component**.\n\n### Key Characteristics of Props:\n\n- **Uni-directional Data Flow:**\n    - Props flow in one direction only—top-down from parent to child.\n- **Immutable:**\n    - A child component can **read** props but **cannot modify** them directly.\n- **HTML Attribute Syntax:**\n    - Props are passed to components using a syntax similar to standard HTML attributes.\n- **Availability:**\n    - Available in both functional components (as function arguments) and class components (via `this.props`).\n\n### Code Example: Passing & Receiving Props\n\n#### Functional Component (Modern Standard using Destructuring):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// Child Component\nconst ChildProps = (props) => {\n    let{data1, data2, data3} = props;\n    // console.log(data1);\n    // console.log(data2);\n    // console.log(data3);\n    return(\n        <div>\n            <h1>ChildProps</h1>\n            <h2>String : {data1}</h2>\n            <h2>Array : {data2}</h2>\n            <h3>Object_ID : {data3.id}</h3>\n            <h3>Object_Name : {data3.objName}</h3>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\nexport default ChildProps;\n\n// Parent Component\n\nimport ChildProps from \"./ChildProps\";\n\nconst ParentProp = () =>{\n\n    let str = \"Bhawesh\";\n    let skills = [\"HTML\", \"CSS\", \"JS\"]\n    let obj = {\n        id : 1,\n        objName : \"Kumar\",\n        address : {\n            pin : 813106,\n            street : \"XYZ\"\n        }\n    }\n    return (\n\n        <div>\n            <h1>ParentProp</h1>\n            <ChildProps data = {str} data2 = {skills} data3= {obj} />\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default ParentProp\n```\n\n## 2. Props Drilling\n\n- Passing the data from one components to nested child components is known as PROPS DRILLING.\n- **Props Drilling** occurs when data is passed through multiple layers of nested intermediate components that do **not** actually need the data themselves, just to reach a deeply nested child component.\n\n```jsx\n[ App Component (Holds State) ]\n            │\n            ▼  (Passes prop down)\n   [ Navigation Component ]      <-- Doesn't use prop!\n            │\n            ▼  (Passes prop down)\n     [ UserMenu Component ]      <-- Doesn't use prop!\n            │\n            ▼  (Passes prop down)\n    [ UserAvatar Component ]     <-- Needs & Uses prop!\n```\n\n### Disadvantages of Props Drilling:\n\n1. **Unnecessary Maintenance Burden:**\n    1.  If the prop name or structure changes, you have to update every intermediate component in the chain.\n2. **Boilerplate & Clutter:** \n    1. Unused props bloat intermediate components and reduce code readability.\n3. **Re-rendering Issues:**\n    1.  Changes to props force updates down the component tree.\n\n### How to Fix Props Drilling:\n\n- To avoid this unnecessary rerandring components we uses one HOOK that is USE CONTAXT (CONTEXT API).\n- To bypass intermediate components, React offers solutions like:\n    - **Context API (`useContext` Hook):**\n        - Provides global state to any deeply nested component without manually passing props at every level.\n    - **State Management Libraries:**\n        - Redux Toolkit, Zustand, or Jotai for complex global application state.\n    \n    ```jsx\n    //Child\n    \n    const Child = ({data}) => {\n    \n        // console.log(props);\n        // let{data} = props;\n        // console.log(data);\n        return(\n            <>\n                <h1>Child : {data}</h1>\n            </>\n        )\n    }\n    export default Child\n    \n    //----\n    \n    //Parent\n    \n    import Child from \"./Child\"\n    \n    const Parent = (props) =>{\n        // console.log(props);\n    \n        let{str} = props;\n        // console.log(str);\n        return(\n            <>\n                <h1>Parent</h1>\n                <Child data={str}/>\n           </>\n        )\n    }\n    \n    export default Parent\n    \n    //-------\n    \n    //Grand Parent\n    \n    import Parent from \"./Parent\"\n    \n    const GrandParent = ()=>{\n        let str = \"MainUser\"\n        return(\n            <>\n                {/* <h1>GrandParent : {str}</h1> */}\n                <h1>GrandParent</h1>\n                <Parent str={str}/>\n            </>\n        )\n    }\n    \n    export default GrandParent\n    \n    ```\n    \n\n## 3. Default Props\n\n- Default Props are the predefined values that a react components usages when a specific props is not provided by the parent components.\n- They act like backup values to ensure the components still works properly even if no values are passed.\n- **Default Props** are predefined fallback values used by a component when a parent component does not pass a specific prop. They ensure that the UI renders predictably without breaking or returning `undefined`.\n\n### Code expampe 1 :-\n\n```jsx\n//DefaultComponents \n\nimport DefaultProps1 from \"./DefaultProps1\"\n\nconst DefaultComponent = ()=>{\n    let data = \"user1\"\n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>Default Components</h1>\n           <DefaultProps1 abc={data}/>\n            <DefaultProps1/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default DefaultComponent\n\n//-----\n\n//DefaultProps 1 \n\nconst DefaultProps1 = ({abc = \"Guest\"})=>{\n    \n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>DefaultProps1 : {abc} </h1>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\nexport default DefaultProps1\n```\n\n### Code expampe 2:-\n\n```jsx\n//DefaultComponents \n\nimport DefaultProps1 from \"./DefaultProps1\"\nimport DefaultProps2 from \"./DefaultProps2\"\n\nconst DefaultComponent = ()=>{\n    let data = \"user1\"\n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>Default Components</h1>\n            {/* <DefaultProps1 abc={data}/>\n            <DefaultProps1/> */}\n\n            <DefaultProps2 data = {data}/>\n            <DefaultProps2/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default DefaultComponent\n\n//----\n\n//DefaultProps 2 \n\n        // function DefaultProps2(props){\nconst DefaultProps2 = (props)=>{\n    \n    let {data} = props;\n    // let {data = \"Guest\"} = props;\n    \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>DefaultProps2 : {data || \"Guest\"} </h1>\n            {/* <h1>DefaultProps2 : {data} </h1> */}\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default DefaultProps2\n\n```\n\n# 🔄 Component Lifecycle & Phases in React\n\n## What is the React Component Lifecycle?\n\n- React Life Cycle means the different states  a components goes through.\n- Every React component goes through a series of stages from the moment it is created and inserted into the DOM to the time it is destroyed and removed. These stages are known as the **Component Lifecycle**.\n\nThe lifecycle is divided into **three primary phases**, plus a specialized **Error Handling** phase.\n\n## The Three Primary Lifecycle Phases\n\n### 1. Mounting Phase (Birth)\n\n- When ever any components render first time in the UI this Phase is called mounting Phase.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Occurs when a component is created and inserted into the Real DOM for the **first time**.\n- **Common Tasks:**\n    - Setting initial state, making API requests to fetch data, setting up subscriptions/event listeners.\n\n### 2. Updating Phase (Growth)\n\n- When ever in state or Props of a components changes and the components gets re-rendering this faces comes under updating Phase\n- **Definition:**\n    - Occurs whenever a component’s **`state`** or **`props`** change, causing React to re-render the component and sync changes with the DOM.\n- **Common Tasks:**\n    - Refetching data when props change, updating UI based on user input, running side effects.\n\n### 3. Unmounting Phase (Death)\n\n- When ever the components is removes from the UI this faces is known as unmounting Phase.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Occurs when a component is **removed/deleted** from the Real DOM tree.\n- **Common Tasks:**\n    - Cleaning up timers, canceling network requests, removing event listeners to prevent **memory leaks**.\n\n## NOTE :- 4. Special Phase: Error Handling\n\n- Error Handling in life cycle is the process where catches the errors in components and prevents the entire app from crashing\n- React Introduced this feature in REACT 16 with error boundaries\n- Introduced in **React 16**, Error Handling uses **Error Boundaries** to catch JavaScript errors anywhere in a child component tree, log those errors, and display a fallback UI instead of crashing the entire application.\n\n> ⚠️ **Note:** Currently, Error Boundaries **must be written as Class Components** because there are no functional hook equivalents for `componentDidCatch` or `getDerivedStateFromError`.\n> \n\n```jsx\n//App.jsx\nimport LifeCycleInCbc from \"./lifeCycle/LifeCycleInCbc\"\nimport { useState } from \"react\"\n\nconst App = () =>{\n\n        let [toggle, setToggle] = useState(false)\n        // // console.log(toggle);\n        \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>App</h1>\n            \n                {/* <LifeCycleInCbc/> */}\n\n            {/* <button onClick={()=> setToggle(!toggle)}>Toggle</button>\n            {toggle ? <LifeCycleInCbc/> : \"No-Component\"} */}\n\n        </div>\n    )\n}\nexport default App\n\n//LifeCycleInCbc.jsx\n\nimport React , {Component} from \"react\";\n\nexport default class LifeCycleInCbc extends Component{\n\n    state = {\n        count : 0\n    }\n\n    componentDidMount(){\n        console.log(\"Mounting Phase\");\n    }\n    componentDidUpdate(){\n        console.log(\"Unmounting Phase\");\n    }\n\n    componentWillUnmount(){\n        console.log(\"Unmounting Phase\");\n    }\n\n    Increment = ()=>{\n        this.setState({count : this.state.count + 1})\n    }\n\n    render(){\n        return(\n            <div>\n                <h1>LifeCycleInCbc : {this.state.count}</h1>\n\n                <button onClick={this.Increment}>Increment</button>\n            </div>\n        )\n    }\n}\n```\n\n# ⚡ React Hooks: `useEffect`\n\n## What is `useEffect`?\n\n- Use Effect is a react hook which helps to handle side effect in our react application.\n- This Hook Can also we used for checking the Phases of Function Based Components.\n- `useEffect` is a core **React Hook** used to perform **side effects** in functional components.\n\n### What is a \"Side Effect\"?\n\n- Side effect live fetching the data from API removing Event-Listener, Updating DOM etc.\n- A side effect is any operation that affects something outside the scope of the current function execution, such as:\n    - **Fetching data** from an external API (`fetch` / `axios`).\n    - **Setting up or removing** timers (`setInterval`, `setTimeout`).\n    - **Directly manipulating** the Real DOM (e.g., updating `document.title`).\n    - **Adding or removing** window event listeners (`scroll`, `resize`).\n\n#### `useEffect` accepts **two arguments**:\n\n1. **Callback Function:** \n2. **Dependency Array:**\n    1. **Callback Function:** \n        1. Contains the side-effect logic to execute. \n        2. It can optionally return a **cleanup function** to cancel subscriptions, clear timers, or remove event listeners before the component re-renders or unmounts.\n    2. **Dependency Array (Optional):**\n        1.  Controls **when** the callback function executes based on value changes.\n\n## Syntax & Arguments\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  // 1. Effect Code (Side Effect Logic)\n\n  return () => {\n    // 2. Cleanup Function (Optional)\n  };\n}, [dependencies]); // 3. Dependency Array (Optional)\n```\n\nCODE :-\n\n```jsx\n//App.jsx\nimport LifeCycleInFbc from \"./lifeCycle/LifeCycleInFbc\"\nimport { useState } from \"react\"\n\nconst App = () =>{\n\n        // let [toggle, setToggle] = useState(false)\n        // // console.log(toggle);\n        \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>App</h1>\n\n               <LifeCycleInFbc/> \n                \n           <button onClick={()=> setToggle(!toggle)}>Toggle</button>\n            {toggle ? <LifeCycleInFbc/> : \"No-Component\"}\n\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default App\n\n//LifeCycleInFbc.jsx\n\nimport { useEffect, useState } from \"react\";\n\n const LifeCycleInFbc = () =>{\n\n    let [count, setCount] = useState(0)\n\n    useEffect(()=>{\n        console.log(\"Updation Phase\");\n    },[])\n\n    useEffect(()=>{\n        console.log(\"Updation Phase\");\n    },[count])\n\n    useEffect(()=>{\n        return ()=>{\n            console.log(\"Un-Mounting Phase\");\n        }\n    },[])\n\n    return(\n        <div>\n            <h1>LifeCycleInFbc : {count}</h1>\n            <button onClick={()=>setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default LifeCycleInFbc\n```\n\n//—————\n**Lifecycle Implementation: Class Components vs. Functional Components (`useEffect`)**\n\n| **Lifecycle Phase** | **Class Component Method** | **Functional Component Equivalent (useEffect)** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Mounting** | `componentDidMount()` | `useEffect(() => { ... }, [])` *(Empty dependency array)* |\n| **Updating** | `componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState)` | `useEffect(() => { ... }, [dependency])` *(With dependencies)* |\n| **Unmounting** | `componentWillUnmount()` | `useEffect(() => { return () => { ... } }, [])` *(Cleanup function)* |\n| **Error Handling** | `componentDidCatch()` / `getDerivedStateFromError()` | Class Component Error Boundary |\n\n# 🎨 React Hooks: `useLayoutEffect`\n\n## What is `useLayoutEffect`?\n\n- Use layout effect is a react hook that runs synchronically after the DOM has been updated, but before the browser paints the screen.\n- `useLayoutEffect` is a version of `useEffect` that fires **synchronously** after React has performed all DOM mutations, but **before the browser paints the updated screen to the user**.\n- `useLayoutEffect` accepts the **exact same arguments** as `useEffect`:\n- It accepts two arguments :\n    1. CallBack Function :\n    2. Dependency Array :\n        1.  **Callback Function:** \n            1. Contains the DOM manipulation/measurement logic and optional cleanup function.\n        2. **Dependency Array:** \n            1. Controls when the effect re-runs.\n\n## Key Difference: `useEffect` vs. `useLayoutEffect`\n\n```\n[ State Changes / Render ]\n           │\n           ▼\n[ React Mutates Virtual DOM ]\n           │\n           ▼\n[ useLayoutEffect Runs ] ───► (Synchronous: Reads/Measures DOM before user sees it)\n           │\n           ▼\n[ Browser Paints Screen ]\n           │\n           ▼\n[ useEffect Runs ] ─────────► (Asynchronous: Runs after screen is visible)\n```\n\n| **Feature** | **useEffect** | **useLayoutEffect** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Execution Timing** | **Asynchronous** (Runs *after* browser paints screen) | **Synchronous** (Runs *before* browser paints screen) |\n| **User Experience** | Fast, doesn't block painting | Blocks painting until execution finishes |\n| **Visual Flickering** | Can cause visible flickering if DOM is modified | **Prevents flickering** by modifying DOM before visual paint |\n| **Primary Use Cases** | API fetching, event listeners, state updates | DOM measurements (width/height), tooltip positioning, layout adjustments |\n\n## Syntax & Arguments\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\n  // Synchronous DOM manipulation or measurements\n\n  return () => {\n    // Cleanup logic before unmount/re-render\n  };\n}, [dependencies]);\n```\n\n## When to Use `useLayoutEffect`\n\nUse `useLayoutEffect` **only when you need to read the layout from the DOM and immediately re-render before the user sees the screen**:\n\n1. **Measuring DOM Elements:** \n    1. Calculating `getBoundingClientRect()`, element width, or element height before rendering tooltips or popups.\n2. **Preventing Visual Flickers:** \n    1. Adjusting scroll positions, element positions, or inline styles before the browser paints.\n\n> ⚠️ **Performance Warning:** Because `useLayoutEffect` is synchronous, long-running code inside it will **block the browser from rendering**, causing noticeable page lag. Default to `useEffect` whenever possible!\n> \n\n## Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n//App.jsx\n\nimport { useState } from \"react\"\n\nimport UseEffect from \"./UseEffect/UseEffect\"\nimport UseLayout from \"./useLayoutEffect/UseLayout\"\n\nconst App = () =>{\n        \n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h1>App</h1>\n            <UseEffect/>\n            <UseLayout/>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default App\n\n// UseLayout\nimport React, { useEffect, useLayoutEffect, useState } from 'react'\n\nconst UseLayout = () => {\n\n    let[data, setData] = useState(\"JavaScript\")\n\n    // useEffect(()=>{\n    //     setData(\"ReactJS\")\n    // },[]) //runs after the screen paint\n\n    useLayoutEffect(()=>{\n        setData(\"ReactJS\")\n    },[]) //runs before the screen paint\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>UseLayout : {data}</h1>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default UseLayout\n```\n\n# 🎨 Ways of Adding CSS in React\n\nIn React, there are several approaches to style components depending on project size and scoping requirements.\n\n1. Inline CSS\n2. Internal CSS\n3. External CSS\n\n## 1. Inline CSS\n\n- **Definition:**\n    - Writing styles directly on JSX elements using the `style` attribute as a **JavaScript object**.\n    - In inline CSS Properties are written in camel case and values are written mostly in string.\n- **Syntax Rules:**\n    - Property names are written in **camelCase** (e.g., `backgroundColor` instead of `background-color`).\n    - Property values are typically passed as **strings** (e.g., `'16px'`, `'center'`). Numeric values without units default to pixels (`padding: 10` becomes `10px`).\n\n### Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction InlineCard(){\n  // Can be passed directly or as a defined style object\n  const cardStyle = {\n    backgroundColor: '#f4f4f4',\n    padding: '20px',\n    borderRadius: '8px',\n    textAlign: 'center'\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div style={cardStyle}>\n      <h2 style={{ color: '#007bff', fontSize: '24px' }}>Inline Styling</h2>\n      <p>Styles are applied directly as JS objects.</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InlineCard;\n```\n\n## 2. Internal CSS (Using `<style>` Tag)\n\n- Internal CSS is define inside the same file or components using a style tag in JSX.\n- it is used for small components, where CSS is not reused everywhere.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Defining CSS rules inside the component file using a `<style>` tag within JSX or template strings.\n- **Usage:**\n    - Useful for quick prototyping or isolated small components, though rarely used in large production codebases due to global scope leakage unless paired with CSS-in-JS tools.\n\n### Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nfunction InternalCard(){\n  return (\n    <div className=\"internal-container\">\n    \n      <style>{`\n      \n        .internal-container {\n          background-color: #eef2f7;\n          border: 1px solid #d1d5db;\n          padding: 15px;\n          border-radius: 6px;\n        }\n        \n        .internal-container h3 {\n          color: #1f2937;\n        }\n        \n        .example1\n                 {\n                        color : red;\n                        font-size : 100px;\n                        background-color : black;\n                        text-align : center;\n                  }\n                  \n          .example2\n                    {\n                               color : white;\n                        font-size : 100px;\n                        background-color : black;\n                        text-align : center;\n                    }\n        \n      `}\n      </style>\n      \n      <h3 className='example1'>Internal CSS Example</h3>\n      <p className='example2'>Styles defined directly inside a JSX style block.</p>\n      \n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InternalCard;\n```\n\n## 3. External CSS\n\n- In external CSS we have to create separate CSS file with . CSS extension and then we have to import the CSS file in our components.\n- **Definition:**\n    - Creating a separate stylesheet with a `.css` extension and importing it directly into the component file using ES6 `import`.\n- **Behavior:**\n    - By default, standard external CSS is **globally scoped**, meaning class names can clash across different components if names overlap.\n\n### Code Example:\n\n**`Card.css`** (External Stylesheet)\n\nCSS\n\n```css\n.external-card {\n  background-color: #ffffff;\n  border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;\n  padding: 20px;\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05);\n}\n\n.external-card .title {\n  color: #111827;\n  font-size: 1.25rem;\n}\n```\n\n**`Card.jsx`** (Component File)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport './Card.css'; // Import the external stylesheet\n\nfunction Card(){\n  return (\n    <div className=\"external-card\">\n      <h3 className=\"title\">External CSS Example</h3>\n      <p>Imported from an external .css file.</p>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Card;\n```\n\n# 📦 CSS Modules in React\n\n## What is a CSS Module?\n\n- CSS module allows you to scope CSS locally to a components avoiding global conflicts.\n- Each class gets a unique generated name.\n- In Big react Project multiple Developer can Style Components without Overriding Global CSS\n- A **CSS Module** is a CSS file where all class names and animation names are **scoped locally** to the component that imports it by default, avoiding **global scope conflicts** and accidental style overrides.\n\n## Why Use CSS Modules?\n\n1. **Local Scoping (No Class Name Collisions):**\n    1.  In large React projects with multiple developers, two developers can create a class named `.button` or `.container` without their styles overwriting each other.\n2. **Unique Generated Names:** \n    1. During build time, the bundler (Vite / Webpack) converts your class names into unique hashed strings (e.g., `button` ——> `_Button_button_abc123`).\n3. **Seamless Integration:** \n    1. Built into modern build tools like **Vite** and **Create React App** out of the box—no extra configuration needed.\n\n## How It Works (Naming Convention)\n\n- To tell React and the module bundler to treat a stylesheet as a module, name the file using the format:\n\n$$\n\\text{filename}\\mathbf{.module.css}\n$$\n\n## Step-by-Step Code Example\n\n### 1. Create the Module Stylesheet: `Button.module.css`\n\nCSS\n\n```css\n/* Button.module.css */\n\n.container{\n    background-color: aqua;\n    height: 60vh;\n    width: 100vw;\n    display: flex;\n    justify-content: center;\n    align-items: center;\n}\nform{\n    height: 80%;\n    width: 80%;\n    background-color: white;\n    display: flex;\n    flex-direction: column;\n    justify-content: center;\n    align-items: center;\n    gap: 10px;\n    border-radius: 10px;\n}\n.heading{\n    text-align: center;\n}\n\ninput, #btn{\n    padding: 10px;\n    border-radius: 10px;\n    width: 70%;\n    border: none;\n    border: 1px solid rgb(203,201,201);\n\n}\ninput{\n    border-radius: 5px;\n\n}\n#btn{\n    background-color: aqua;\n    color: #871414;\n    font-weight: bold;\n    font-size: 15px;\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Import and Use in Component: `Button.jsx`\n\nImport the CSS module as a JavaScript object (commonly named `styles` or `classes`):\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n// Button.jsx\nimport styles from './Button.module.css';\n\nconst Button = ()=>{\n    return (\n        <div className={style.container}>\n            <form>\n                <h1 className={style.heading}>Login</h1>\n                <input type=\"text\" placeholder='UserName' />\n                <input type=\"password\" placeholder='Password' />\n                <button id={style.btn}> Login </button>\n                \n            </form>\n        </div>\n    )\n}\n\nexport default Button\n```\n\n**Quick Comparison Summary :-**\n\n| **Styling Method** | **Scope** | **Syntax** | **Best Use Case** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Inline CSS** | Component / Element | JS Object (`camelCase`) | Dynamic values calculated at runtime |\n| **Internal CSS** | Global (unless wrapped) | Standard CSS inside `<style>` | Quick demos or single-file prototypes |\n| **External CSS** | Global | Standard CSS (`.css` files) | Global app styles, resets, typography |\n| **CSS Modules** | **Local / Scoped** | Imported as `styles.className` | Component-specific styling in production |\n\n# 🌐 React Context API & `useContext`\n\n## What is Context API?\n\n- CONTEXT API in the react that allows you to share the data globally between different components without passing props manually And each level.\n- Previously before context API we can not share the data directly to the middle components. To pass the data directly to the middle components we have to used context API.\n- Steps to create CONTEXT API\n    - 1. Create CONTEXT.\n    - 2. CONTEXT Providers && Providing Values.\n    - 3. Use CONTEXT.\n- The **Context API** is a built-in React feature designed to share data (state, functions, theme, user authentication) **globally** across an entire component tree without needing to pass props manually through every intermediate component level (**eliminates Props Drilling**).\n\n```jsx\n   Without Context (Props Drilling)           With Context API\n\n        [ App (User State) ]                 [ UserContext.Provider ]\n                 │                                      │\n                 ▼                                      │ (Global Access)\n        [ Dashboard (Prop) ]                            ├──► [ Header ]\n                 │                                      │\n                 ▼                                      └──► [ UserProfile ]\n        [ UserProfile (Prop) ]\n```\n\n## —> 3 Core Steps to Implement Context API\n\n1. **`createContext()`**:\n    1.  Creates the context object.\n2. **`Context.Provider`**:\n    1.  Wraps the component tree and supplies the data (`value` prop) to all nested children.\n3. **`useContext()`**: \n    1. The hook used by any child component to consume the provided data directly.\n\n**CODE EXAMPLE 1 :** \n\n```jsx\n//MyContext.jsx\n\nimport React, { createContext } from 'react'\nconst MyContext = createContext();\nexport default MyContext\n\n// ContextParnet.jsx\n\nimport ContextChild1 from \"./ContextChild1\"\nimport MyContext from \"./MyContext\";\n\nconst ContextParent = () => {\n\n    let data1 = \"Global Data\";\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextParent</h1>\n        <MyContext.Provider value = {data1}>\n\n            <ContextChild1/>\n\n           </MyContext.Provider>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\nexport default ContextParent\n\n//ContextChild1.jsx\n\nimport ContextChild2 from \"./ContextChild2\"\n\nconst ContextChild1 = () => {\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextChild1</h1>\n        <ContextChild2/>\n    </div>\n\n  )\n}\nexport default ContextChild1\n\n// ContextChild2.jsx\n\nimport { useContext } from \"react\"\nimport MyContext from \"./MyContext\"\n\nconst ContextChild2 = () => {\n\n    let data = useContext(MyContext);\n    console.log(data);\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextChild2 : {data}</h1>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextChild2\n\n```\n\n**CODE EXAMPLE 2 :** \n\n```jsx\n//ContextGrandParnet.jsx\n\nimport { createContext } from \"react\"\nimport ContextParent2 from \"./ContextParent2\"\n\n  //! step 1:\n\n   export let MyContext2 = createContext();\n\nconst ContextGrandParent = () => {\n\n    let data2 = \"Global Data 2\"\n    let data3 = \"Global Data 3\"\n    let data4 = \"Global Data 4\"\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextGrandParent</h1>\n\n        {/* //! step 2: */}\n\n        <MyContext2.Provider value = {{data2, data3, data4}}>\n\n            <ContextParent2/>\n\n        </MyContext2.Provider>\n\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextGrandParent\n\n//ContextParnet2.jsx\n\nimport ContextChild from \"./ContextChild\"\n\nconst ContextParent2 = () => {\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextParent2</h1>\n        <ContextChild/>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextParent2\n\n// ContextChild.jsx\n\nimport React, { useContext } from 'react'\nimport {MyContext2} from './ContextGrandParent'\n\nconst ContextChild = () => {\n\n    let ContextData = useContext(MyContext2)\n    let {data2,data3,data4} = ContextData\n    // console.log(ContextData);\n    \n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>ContextChild : {data2}  </h1>\n        <h1>ContextChild : {data3}  </h1>\n        <h1>ContextChild : {data4}  </h1>\n\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default ContextChild\n```\n\n# **⚙️ React Hooks: `useReducer`**\n\n## What is `useReducer`?\n\n- Use Reducer is a react Hooks which is used to handle complex state logic\n- It accepts two arguments :\n    - **Reducer Function**\n    - **Initial State**\n- It is an alternative to `useState` that follows the Redux-style pattern: actions trigger state transitions via a centralized reducer function.\n\n## Hook Syntax & Arguments\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nconst [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);\n```\n\n## 1.  `Reducer Function`:\n\n- It is the function that decided how state changes\n- **`reducer` (Reducer Function):** A custom function that determines how the state transitions.\n- It accepts two arguments:\n    \n    #### `a. state`:\n    \n    - The current/previous state.\n    \n    #### `b. action`:  ****\n    \n    - **An object dispatched to request a state change (typically has a `type` and optional `payload`).**\n- *Returns:*  It returns a new Updated State.\n\n## 2.  `Initial State`:\n\n- It is also known as initial value that we wants our state should Hold.\n- The starting value/object that the state holds.\n- It returns an array which consists two values.\n    1.  Variable which hold the current state.\n    2.  Function which is known as this Dispatcher function\n    - **`current state` (Index 0):**\n        - The current state variable.\n    - **`dispatch fn` (Index 1):**\n        - The dispatch function used to trigger actions and send them to the reducer function.\n\n## How `useReducer` Works Under the Hood\n\n```jsx\n[ User Interaction ] ──► dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n[ Reducer Function ] ──► reducer(currentState, action)\n                                  │\n                                  ▼\n[ New State Returned ] ─► Component Re-renders with Updated State\n```\n\n## Code Examples\n\n### 1. Simple Counter Example\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\nimport { useReducer } from 'react';\n\n// 1. Define Initial State\nconst initialState = { count: 0 };\n\n// 2. Define Reducer Function\nfunction counterReducer(state, action){\n  switch (action.type) {\n    case 'INCREMENT':\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\n    case 'DECREMENT':\n      return { count: state.count - 1 };\n    case 'RESET':\n      return { count: 0 };\n    default:\n      throw new Error(`Unhandled action type: ${action.type}`);\n  }\n}\n\n// 3. Component\nexport default function Counter(){\n  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(counterReducer, initialState);\n\n  return (\n    <div style={{ padding: '20px', fontFamily: 'sans-serif' }}>\n      <h2>Count: {state.count}</h2>\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' })}>Increment</button>\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'DECREMENT' })}>Decrement</button>\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'RESET' })}>Reset</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## When to Use `useState` vs. `useReducer`\n\n| **Criteria** | **useState** | **useReducer** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Data Type** | Primitives (`number`, `string`, `boolean`) | Complex objects, arrays, nested state |\n| **State Transitions** | Simple, independent updates (1–2 values) | Multiple sub-values or interdependent state logic |\n| **Business Logic** | Kept directly inside event handlers | Centralized inside a single, pure reducer function |\n| **Testing** | Harder to test state logic in isolation | Pure reducer functions are easy to unit-test independently |\n\n# 🎛️ Controlled vs. Uncontrolled Components & `useRef`\n\n- Forms are essential in web applications. In React, there are two distinct paradigms for handling form input elements (`<input>`, `<textarea>`, `<select>`): **Controlled Components** and **Uncontrolled Components**.\n\n## 1. Controlled Components\n\n- A control components is a components in which the input elements get their values from react State and any changes in the input is handle through event handler like onChange.\n- **Definition:**\n    - A component where the form input element's value is **driven and controlled by React state** (`useState`).\n- **Mechanism:**\n    1. The input's `value` attribute is bound to a state variable.\n    2. Any keystroke or user input triggers an `onChange` event handler to update that state.\n- **Source of Truth:**\n    - **React State** is the single source of truth.\n- **Best Used For:**\n    - Real-time form validation, disabling submit buttons conditionally, dynamic inputs, or instant UI updates.\n\n### Controlled Component Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```jsx\n//---------------------- EX 1\n\nimport React, { useState } from 'react'\n\nconst Controlled = () => {\n\n        let [formData, setFormData] = useState({\n\n            userName : \"\",\n            password : \"\",\n            email : \"\"\n        })\n\n        function handleSubmit(e){\n            e.preventDefault()\n\n            console.log(\"Form Submitted\");\n        }\n\n        function handleChange(e){\n            // console.log(e);\n\n            let{name,value} = e.target\n\n            setFormData ({...formData, [name]:value})\n            console.log(formData);\n            \n            console.log(\"Change\");\n        }\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n        <h1>Controlled Components</h1>\n\n        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n\n            <input type=\"text\" value = {FormData.userName} onChange = {handleChange} name = \"userName\" />\n            \n            <input type=\"password\" value = {FormData.password} onChange={handleChange} name = \"password\"/>\n            \n            <input type=\"email\" value ={FormData.email} onChange={handleChange} name = \"email\"/>\n\n            <button>Submit</button>\n        </form>\n\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n\nexport default Controlled\n\n//---------------------- Ex 2 ONLINE\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nexport default function ControlledForm(){\n  const [name, setName] = useState('');\n\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    alert(`Submitted name: ${name}`);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n      <label>Controlled Input: </label>\n      <input\n       type=\"text\" value={name} onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)} placeholder=\"Type here...\" />\n      <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>\n      <p>Live Preview: {name}</p>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Uncontrolled Components\n\n- An Uncontrol components in which input elements manages their own data using the Browsers DOM and react access the values only when required using ref.\n- \n- **Definition:**\n    - A component where the form input element manages its own internal state using the **browser's DOM**.\n- **Mechanism:**\n    - React does not track every keystroke. Instead, React pulls the current value directly from the DOM on demand (e.g., when the user clicks \"Submit\") using a **`useRef`** reference.\n- **Source of Truth:**\n    - The **Browser's Real DOM**.\n- **Best Used For:**\n    - Simple forms that only need values on submit, file input fields (`<input type=\"file\" />`), or integrating with third-party non-React DOM libraries.\n\n### Uncontrolled Component Code Example:\n\nJavaScript\n\n```\nimport { useRef } from 'react';\n\nexport default function UncontrolledForm(){\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\n\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    // Access value directly from the DOM element reference\n    alert(`Submitted name: ${inputRef.current.value}`);\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\n      <label>Uncontrolled Input: </label>\n      <input\n        type=\"text\"\n        ref={inputRef}\n        defaultValue=\"\" // Sets initial value without controlling updates\n        placeholder=\"Type here...\"\n      />\n      <button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## Summary Comparison: Controlled vs. Uncontrolled\n\n| **Feature** | **Controlled Component** | **Uncontrolled Component** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Data Handler** | React State (`useState`) | Real DOM (`useRef`) |\n| **Updates On** | Every keystroke (`onChange`) | On demand / form submit |\n| **Instant Validation** | Easy (available in state on every change) | Harder (requires manual DOM queries) |\n| **Re-renders** | Re-renders on every keystroke | Does **not** re-render on typing |\n| **Standard Recommendation** | **React Recommended** | Useful for edge cases & file uploads |\n\n## 3. The `useRef` Hook\n\n### What is `useRef`?\n\n`useRef` is a built-in React Hook that returns a mutable reference object whose **`.current`** property holds a persisted value across renders.\n\n### Two Core Use Cases of `useRef`:\n\n1. **Accessing and Manipulating DOM Elements Directly:** Focusing an input, scrolling to a section, measuring element dimensions, or playing/pausing media.\n2. **Storing Mutable Values Without Causing Re-renders:** Unlike `useState`, updating `ref.current` **does not trigger a component re-render**.\n\n### Code Examples for `useRef`\n\n#### Use Case 1: Focusing an Input on Button Click (DOM Access)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```\nimport { useRef } from 'react';\n\nexport default function AutoFocusInput(){\n  const inputElement = useRef(null);\n\n  const handleFocus = () => {\n    // Directly focus the input element via the DOM node\n    inputElement.current.focus();\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <input ref={inputElement} type=\"text\" placeholder=\"Click button to focus me\" />\n      <button onClick={handleFocus}>Focus Input</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n#### Use Case 2: Persisting Values Without Re-rendering (Timer / Counter)\n\nJavaScript\n\n```\nimport { useState, useRef } from 'react';\n\nexport default function StopWatch(){\n  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);\n  const timerId = useRef(null); // Holds timer ID without triggering re-renders\n\n  const startTimer = () => {\n    if (timerId.current !== null) return;\n    timerId.current = setInterval(() => {\n      setSeconds((prev) => prev + 1);\n    }, 1000);\n  };\n\n  const stopTimer = () => {\n    clearInterval(timerId.current);\n    timerId.current = null;\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <h2>Seconds: {seconds}</h2>\n      <button onClick={startTimer}>Start</button>\n      <button onClick={stopTimer}>Stop</button>\n    </div>\n  );\n}\n```\n\n## `useState` vs. `useRef`\n\n| **Feature** | **useState** | **useRef** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Triggers Re-render?** | **Yes** (when state updater is called) | **No** (changing `.current` never re-renders) |\n| **Access Syntax** | `stateVariable` | `refVariable.current` |\n| **Primary Purpose** | Visual UI data that dictates component rendering | DOM elements & behind-the-scenes mutable values (timers, previous values) |",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
